PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L08U3.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":8,"cefr":"B2a","unit":3,"theme":"Money Matters","essential_question":"How can smart choices about money shape our future?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["income","expense","savings","invest","profit","loss","entrepreneur","debt","zakat","priority","necessity","luxury","financial","consumer"],"recycled_words":["claim","counterclaim","rebuttal","concede","evidence","statistic","credible","relevant","procedure","call to action","however","moreover","in conclusion"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS1","title":"Word Power: Money Matters","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about money and 6 Tier 2 words (allocate, justify, refute, assert, substantial, verify)","learn and use common finance collocations: verb + noun (*make a profit*), adjective + noun (*monthly income*) and phrasal verbs (*save up*, *pay off*)","choose the correct collocation in context and avoid common wrong combinations such as *do a profit*"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن المال و6 كلمات أكاديمية.","تعلّم واستخدام المتلازمات اللفظية المالية الشائعة: فعل + اسم، وصفة + اسم، والأفعال المركبة.","اختيار المتلازمة الصحيحة في السياق وتجنب التراكيب الخاطئة الشائعة."],"success_criteria":["I can use the money words correctly in sentences.","I can match verbs, adjectives and particles with money nouns to make correct collocations.","I can correct a sentence that uses a wrong collocation."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات المال في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع مطابقة الأفعال والصفات والأدوات مع أسماء المال لتكوين متلازمات صحيحة.","أستطيع تصحيح جملة فيها متلازمة خاطئة."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"income","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnˌkʌm/","definition":"money that you receive regularly from work, a business or other sources","arabic":"دخل","example":"His monthly income comes from his small shop.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"expense","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈspɛns/","definition":"money that you spend on something","arabic":"مصروف / نفقة","example":"Rent is the family's biggest expense.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"savings","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈseɪvɪŋz/","definition":"money that you keep and do not spend","arabic":"مدّخرات","example":"She used her savings to buy a laptop.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"invest","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈvɛst/","definition":"to put money into something to make more money later","arabic":"يستثمر","example":"He invested in his brother's business.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"profit","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɑfɪt/","definition":"money that you gain when you sell something for more than it cost","arabic":"ربح","example":"The stall made a profit of 200 riyals.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"loss","pos":"noun","ipa":"/lɔs/","definition":"money that you lose when costs are more than income","arabic":"خسارة","example":"The shop made a loss in its first month.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"entrepreneur","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɑntrəprəˈnɝ/","definition":"a person who starts a business and takes risks to make it succeed","arabic":"رائد أعمال","example":"The young entrepreneur sold handmade soap.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"debt","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɛt/","definition":"money that you owe to someone","arabic":"دَين","example":"He worked hard to pay off his debt.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"zakat","pos":"noun","ipa":"/zəˈkɑt/","definition":"the obligatory charity in Islam: a share of savings given to people in need","arabic":"الزكاة","example":"Muslims pay zakat on savings they have held for a year.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"priority","pos":"noun","ipa":"/praɪˈɔrəti/","definition":"something that is more important than other things","arabic":"أولوية","example":"Saving for university is my priority.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"necessity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/nəˈsɛsəti/","definition":"something you really need","arabic":"ضرورة","example":"Food and water are necessities.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"luxury","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlʌkʃəri/","definition":"something expensive that is nice to have but not necessary","arabic":"رفاهية / كماليات","example":"A gold watch is a luxury.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"financial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/fəˈnænʃəl/","definition":"connected with money","arabic":"مالي","example":"She set a financial goal for the year.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"consumer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsumər/","definition":"a person who buys goods or services","arabic":"مستهلك","example":"Smart consumers compare prices.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"allocate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈæləˌkeɪt/","definition":"to give a share of something for a particular purpose","arabic":"يخصّص","example":"I allocate 20 riyals a week to savings.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"justify","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈdʒʌstəˌfaɪ/","definition":"to give good reasons for a decision or an opinion","arabic":"يبرّر / يسوّغ","example":"Can you justify spending so much on a phone?","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"refute","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈfjut/","definition":"to prove that a statement or an argument is wrong","arabic":"يدحض / يفنّد","example":"She refuted his claim with clear evidence.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"assert","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈsɝt/","definition":"to state something firmly","arabic":"يؤكّد / يجزم","example":"The writer asserts that saving is a habit.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"substantial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səbˈstænʃəl/","definition":"large in amount or importance","arabic":"كبير / معتبر","example":"He saved a substantial amount in one year.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V19","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V19","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"verify","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈvɛrəˌfaɪ/","definition":"to check that something is true or correct","arabic":"يتحقّق من","example":"Always verify a price before you pay.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V20","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V20","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **collocation** is a group of words that native speakers naturally use together. With money, the wrong verb sounds strange even when the meaning is clear: we **make** a profit (not *do* a profit), **earn** an income, **pay** zakat, **cut** expenses and **run** a business. Learn collocations as **chunks**, not single words.\n\nThree useful patterns: **Verb + noun** – *make a profit / make a loss, earn an income, spend money on, save money for, invest in, pay off a debt, cut costs, set a goal, take a risk*. **Adjective + noun** – *monthly income, financial goal, substantial savings, unexpected expense, smart consumer, small business*. **Phrasal verbs** – *save up (for), pay back, pay off, run out of (money), put aside, cut back on*.\n\nStrategy: when you meet a money noun, **notice the words around it** and write the whole chunk in your vocabulary notebook. When you write, ask: *Which verb normally goes with this noun?* If you are not sure, **verify** it in a learner's dictionary – collocation examples are listed under the noun. SAT-style *words in context* questions often test whether a word fits naturally and precisely.","examples":["Verb + noun: ***The school market made a profit of 350 riyals.***","Verb + noun: ***Ahmed earns a small income by tutoring younger students.***","Verb + noun: ***The family decided to cut expenses by cooking at home.***","Verb + preposition: ***She invested in a sewing machine to start her business.***","Adjective + noun: ***A broken phone screen was an unexpected expense.***","Phrasal verb: ***Khalid is saving up for a bicycle.***","Phrasal verb: ***It took my uncle two years to pay off his debt.***","Phrasal verb: ***We ran out of money before the end of the trip.***","Religious practice: ***Muslims pay zakat on savings that they have held for a full lunar year.***"],"common_errors":["✗ We did a big profit. → ✓ We made a big profit. (make a profit.)","✗ He gains a monthly salary of 4,000 riyals. → ✓ He earns a monthly salary of 4,000 riyals. (earn an income / a salary.)","✗ She spent all her money in clothes. → ✓ She spent all her money on clothes. (spend money **on**.)","✗ They invested on a new shop. → ✓ They invested in a new shop. (invest **in**.)","✗ I am saving for to buy a laptop. → ✓ I am saving up to buy a laptop. / I am saving up for a laptop. (save up **for** + noun / **to** + verb.)","✗ He paid out his debt. → ✓ He paid off his debt. (pay **off** a debt.)","✗ The moneys of the family → ✓ the family's money (*money* is uncountable.)"],"arabic":"المتلازمة اللفظية مجموعة كلمات يستخدمها الناطقون معًا بشكل طبيعي. ومع كلمات المال يبدو الفعل الخاطئ غريبًا وإن كان المعنى واضحًا: نقول make a profit لا do a profit، وearn an income، وpay zakat، وcut expenses، وrun a business. احفظ المتلازمات قطعًا كاملة.\n\nثلاثة أنماط مفيدة: فعل + اسم مثل make a profit وpay off a debt، وصفة + اسم مثل monthly income وunexpected expense، وأفعال مركبة مثل save up وpay back وrun out of.\n\nالاستراتيجية: عند مقابلة اسم مالي لاحظ الكلمات حوله واكتب القطعة كاملة في دفترك، وعند الكتابة اسأل: أي فعل يأتي عادة مع هذا الاسم؟ وتحقق في معجم المتعلمين عند الشك.\n\nانتبه: spend money on وinvest in، وmoney اسم غير معدود."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Every riyal tells a story. Where does your money come from – and where does it go?","on_screen":"Money Matters 💰","visual":"Coins flowing in from a job and out to a shop, a savings jar and charity."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Income, expense, savings, invest, profit, loss, entrepreneur, debt, zakat, priority, necessity, luxury, financial, consumer!","on_screen":"income · expense · savings · invest · profit · loss · entrepreneur · debt · zakat · priority · necessity · luxury · financial · consumer","visual":"Each word appears on a coin dropping into a jar."},{"time":"01:30–02:10","narration":"Allocate your money, justify your choices, verify the facts – and refute weak arguments!","on_screen":"allocate · justify · refute · assert · substantial · verify","visual":"A student dividing a pie chart and checking a receipt."},{"time":"02:10–03:30","narration":"We MAKE a profit, EARN an income, SPEND money ON, INVEST IN and PAY OFF a debt.","on_screen":"Collocations: words that go together","visual":"Verb tiles snapping onto noun tiles like puzzle pieces."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Learn money words in chunks – and check in a dictionary when you're not sure!","on_screen":"Learn chunks, not single words","visual":"A notebook with color-coded chunks."}],"captions_ar":["كل ريال يحكي قصة: من أين يأتي مالك وإلى أين يذهب؟","دخل، مصروف، مدخرات، يستثمر، ربح، خسارة، رائد أعمال، دَين، زكاة، أولوية، ضرورة، رفاهية، مالي، مستهلك!","خصّص مالك، وبرّر اختياراتك، وتحقق من الحقائق، وفنّد الحجج الضعيفة!","نقول make a profit وearn an income وspend money on وinvest in وpay off a debt.","تعلّم كلمات المال قطعًا كاملة، وارجع إلى المعجم عند الشك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, upbeat, friendly male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct money word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة المال الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"Food, water and a safe home are ___, not luxuries.","answer":"necessities","explanation":"Necessity.","explanation_ar":"ضرورات.","options":["profits","necessities","debts","consumers"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A young ___ opened a small online shop selling handmade bags.","answer":"entrepreneur","explanation":"Entrepreneur.","explanation_ar":"رائد أعمال.","options":["expense","luxury","debt","entrepreneur"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Every year, Muslims give ___ from savings they have held for a full lunar year.","answer":"zakat","explanation":"Zakat.","explanation_ar":"الزكاة.","options":["zakat","profit","income","luxury"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The shop sold fewer items than expected and made a ___ in its first month.","answer":"loss","explanation":"Loss.","explanation_ar":"خسارة.","options":["priority","consumer","loss","necessity"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Smart ___ compare prices in several shops before they buy.","answer":"consumers","explanation":"Consumer.","explanation_ar":"المستهلكون.","options":["incomes","debts","consumers","savings"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Paying back the money he owed was his first ___.","answer":"priority","explanation":"Priority.","explanation_ar":"أولوية.","options":["luxury","loss","profit","priority"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct collocation.","instructions_ar":"اختر المتلازمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The students' bake sale ___ a profit of 450 riyals.","answer":"made","explanation":"make a profit.","explanation_ar":"حقق.","options":["made","did","took","gave"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Lina ___ a small income by designing posters for local shops.","answer":"earns","explanation":"earn an income.","explanation_ar":"تكسب.","options":["gains","earns","wins","makes up"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"My father finally ___ the debt on his car last month.","answer":"paid off","explanation":"pay off a debt.","explanation_ar":"سدّد.","options":["paid out","paid on","paid up for","paid off"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses the preposition after *invest* correctly?","answer":"Many families invest in their children's education.","explanation":"invest in.","explanation_ar":"invest in.","options":["Many families invest on their children's education.","Many families invest in their children's education.","Many families invest at their children's education.","Many families invest to their children's education."],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *After the factory closed, the town's residents had to ______ on everything from electricity to transport until new jobs arrived.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise phrase?","answer":"cut back","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"يقلّصوا الإنفاق.","options":["cut back","save up","pay off","invest in"],"dok":3,"skill":"words in context","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Before believing an online advertisement that promises easy riches, a careful consumer should ______ its claims by checking trusted sources.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?","answer":"verify","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"يتحقق.","options":["assert","allocate","verify","invest"],"dok":3,"skill":"words in context","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Omar is saving ________ for a new bicycle.","answer":"up","explanation":"save up for.","explanation_ar":"يدّخر.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"We ran ________ of money before the end of the holiday.","answer":"out","explanation":"run out of.","explanation_ar":"نفد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Families can ________ expenses by turning off unused lights.","answer":"cut","explanation":"cut expenses.","explanation_ar":"يقلّصوا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"I ________ a quarter of my allowance to savings every week.","answer":"allocate","explanation":"Give a share.","explanation_ar":"أخصّص.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The company made a ________ profit, more than double last year's.","answer":"substantial","explanation":"Large.","explanation_ar":"كبيرًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Can you ________ buying a third pair of shoes this month?","answer":"justify","explanation":"Give good reasons.","explanation_ar":"تبرّر.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with collocations.","instructions_ar":"اكتب بالمتلازمات.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with *make a profit* about a school activity.","answer":"Our class made a profit of 300 riyals at the school market, and we gave it to charity.","explanation":"Collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة.","dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a sentence with *save up for* and a sentence with *spend money on*.","answer":"I am saving up for a tablet. Last year I spent too much money on snacks.","explanation":"Collocations.","explanation_ar":"متلازمات.","dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Correct the collocations: *He did a big loss and gained no income, so he paid out his debt late.*","answer":"He made a big loss and earned no income, so he paid off his debt late.","explanation":"Correct.","explanation_ar":"صحّح.","dok":3,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the difference between a necessity and a luxury, with one example of each.","answer":"A necessity is something you really need, such as food or school books. A luxury is nice to have but not necessary, such as an expensive gaming chair.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Collocation Market","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Market trader: a money noun appears on a stall; the child spends coins to buy the correct partner tile (make + profit, pay off + debt). Correct chunks earn profit; wrong chunks cause a loss. Players with the biggest profit pay zakat to unlock the next market.","Each team starts with 50 play riyals.","A team draws a noun card and has 15 seconds to buy a partner tile.","Correct collocation = earn 10 riyals; wrong = lose 5 riyals.","A team that uses the chunk in a full sentence earns 5 bonus riyals.","At the end, teams pay 2.5% zakat and announce their profit."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *money that you owe to someone*?","answer":"debt","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"دَين.","options":["profit","income","debt","savings"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which definition BEST explains a *collocation* such as *make a profit*?","answer":"a group of words that are naturally used together","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة لفظية.","options":["a word with the opposite meaning","a group of words that are naturally used together","a long paragraph","a type of bank"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"*He saved a **substantial** amount of money.* *Substantial* means ___.","answer":"large","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"كبير.","options":["very small","stolen","borrowed","large"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which verb goes with *a profit*?","answer":"make","explanation":"Collocation.","explanation_ar":"make.","options":["do","take","win","make"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *spend* with the correct preposition?","answer":"She spends a lot of money on books.","explanation":"spend on.","explanation_ar":"spend on.","options":["She spends a lot of money on books.","She spends a lot of money in books.","She spends a lot of money for books.","She spends a lot of money at books."],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Which phrasal verb means *to finish paying all the money you owe*?","answer":"pay off","explanation":"Phrasal verbs.","explanation_ar":"pay off.","options":["run out of","save up","pay off","cut back on"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Which item is a LUXURY rather than a necessity?","answer":"a diamond phone case","explanation":"Necessity vs. luxury.","explanation_ar":"رفاهية.","options":["drinking water","a diamond phone case","a school uniform","medicine"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *money* correctly?","answer":"We need more money for the trip.","explanation":"Uncountable noun.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We need more money for the trip.","We need more moneys for the trip.","We need a money for the trip.","We need many money for the trip."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Critics of the new shopping app ______ that it encourages young users to spend money they do not have.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?","answer":"assert","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"يؤكدون.","options":["allocate","verify","invest","assert"],"dok":3,"skill":"words in context"},{"q":"Why is it useful to learn money words as chunks?","answer":"Because the right word partners make writing sound natural and precise.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"تجعل الكتابة طبيعية ودقيقة.","options":["Because single words are never used with money.","Because chunks have no meaning.","Because the right word partners make writing sound natural and precise.","Because dictionaries do not include money words."],"dok":3,"skill":"collocations"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'income': a hand receiving a pay envelope"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'expense': a receipt with a list of costs"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'savings': a jar filling with coins"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'invest': a coin planted in soil growing into a plant"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'profit': a graph line going up with a coin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'loss': a graph line going down"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'entrepreneur': a young person opening a small shop"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'debt': a note saying 'I owe you 500'"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'zakat': a hand giving money to a family in need"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'priority': a list with the first item circled"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'necessity': bread, water and medicine"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'luxury': a shiny gold watch"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'financial': a notebook with money goals"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'consumer': a shopper comparing two price tags"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'allocate': a cake divided into labeled slices"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'justify': a person explaining with raised hand and reasons"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'refute': a debater pointing to a chart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'assert': a speaker speaking confidently at a podium"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V19","prompt":"Picture for the word 'substantial': a tall stack of coins"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V20","prompt":"Picture for the word 'verify': a magnifying glass over a receipt with a check mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-01","prompt":"Planning how to use money wisely."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for money words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS1-03","prompt":"Money words that go together."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V01","script":"income. His monthly income comes from his small shop."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V02","script":"expense. Rent is the family's biggest expense."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V03","script":"savings. She used her savings to buy a laptop."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V04","script":"invest. He invested in his brother's business."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V05","script":"profit. The stall made a profit of 200 riyals."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V06","script":"loss. The shop made a loss in its first month."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V07","script":"entrepreneur. The young entrepreneur sold handmade soap."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V08","script":"debt. He worked hard to pay off his debt."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V09","script":"zakat. Muslims pay zakat on savings they have held for a year."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V10","script":"priority. Saving for university is my priority."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V11","script":"necessity. Food and water are necessities."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V12","script":"luxury. A gold watch is a luxury."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V13","script":"financial. She set a financial goal for the year."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V14","script":"consumer. Smart consumers compare prices."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V15","script":"allocate. I allocate 20 riyals a week to savings."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V16","script":"justify. Can you justify spending so much on a phone?"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V17","script":"refute. She refuted his claim with clear evidence."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V18","script":"assert. The writer asserts that saving is a habit."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V19","script":"substantial. He saved a substantial amount in one year."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-V20","script":"verify. Always verify a price before you pay."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS1-VID","script":"Every riyal tells a story. Where does your money come from – and where does it go? Income, expense, savings, invest, profit, loss, entrepreneur, debt, zakat, priority, necessity, luxury, financial, consumer! Allocate your money, justify your choices, verify the facts – and refute weak arguments! We MAKE a profit, EARN an income, SPEND money ON, INVEST IN and PAY OFF a debt. Learn money words in chunks – and check in a dictionary when you're not sure!"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2","title":"Financial Education Should Be Compulsory","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read an argument essay of about 665 words and identify its claim, reasons, evidence, counterclaims and rebuttals","explain how statistics from a credible source support each reason","evaluate the strength of the writer's rebuttals and the effect of the conclusion"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقالة جدلية من نحو 665 كلمة وتحديد ادعائها وأسبابها وأدلتها والادعاءات المضادة والردود.","شرح كيف تدعم الإحصاءات من مصدر موثوق كل سبب.","تقويم قوة ردود الكاتب وأثر الخاتمة."],"success_criteria":["I can state the writer's claim and three reasons in my own words.","I can match each statistic to the reason it supports and name its source.","I can explain how the writer answers two counterclaims."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر ادعاء الكاتب وأسبابه الثلاثة بكلماتي.","أستطيع ربط كل إحصائية بالسبب الذي تدعمه وذكر مصدرها.","أستطيع شرح كيف يرد الكاتب على ادعاءين مضادين."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"compulsory","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kəmˈpʌlsəri/","definition":"that must be done because of a rule or a law","arabic":"إلزامي","example":"English is a compulsory subject at our school.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"optional","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɑpʃənəl/","definition":"that you can choose to do or not do","arabic":"اختياري","example":"The art club is optional.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"literacy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlɪtərəsi/","definition":"the knowledge and skills needed in a particular area, such as reading or money","arabic":"إلمام / معرفة أساسية","example":"Financial literacy helps people make smart choices.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"scam","pos":"noun","ipa":"/skæm/","definition":"a dishonest trick to get someone's money","arabic":"احتيال / نصب","example":"The message promising a free phone was a scam.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"assessment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈsɛsmənt/","definition":"a test or a judgment of someone's knowledge or skill","arabic":"تقييم","example":"The PISA assessment tests 15-year-olds.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"timetable","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtaɪmˌteɪbəl/","definition":"a plan that shows when lessons or events happen","arabic":"جدول حصص / جدول زمني","example":"The school timetable is already full.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"critic","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkrɪtɪk/","definition":"a person who says that something is wrong or bad","arabic":"ناقد / معارض","example":"Critics say the course would take time from other subjects.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **argument essay** tries to convince readers to accept a **claim** (*Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject*). The claim is usually stated in the introduction as a **thesis**, often with a **preview of reasons** (*because…, because… and because…*). Each body paragraph develops **one reason**, supported by **evidence**: statistics, facts, examples or expert sources.\n\nStrong writers also deal with the other side. A **counterclaim** is an opposing view (*Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents*). A **rebuttal** answers it. Notice the moves: the writer often **concedes** a fair point (*It is true that families play an important role*) and then **turns** with *However, Yet, Moreover* to show why the claim still stands.\n\nAs a close reader, ask: *What is the claim? What are the reasons? Which evidence supports which reason? Is the source named and credible? Are the counterclaims answered fairly?* Also notice the **conclusion**: it restates the claim in new words and often ends with a memorable line (*not a luxury; it is a necessity*).","examples":["Claim + preview of reasons: ***Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because…***","Hook: ***Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations … but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget.***","Statistic + named source: ***According to the OECD, … about 60% of the students … had a bank account or a payment card.***","Explaining a statistic: ***In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems.***","Linking evidence to behavior: ***high performers … were 72% more likely than low performers to save money***","Counterclaim: ***Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools.***","Concession: ***It is true that families play an important role.***","Rebuttal: ***However, not every parent feels confident about money.***","Memorable conclusion: ***Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Confusing the topic with the claim. → ✓ Topic: financial education. Claim: it should be compulsory. (Claim = a position.)","✗ Thinking the counterclaim is the writer's opinion. → ✓ The counterclaim is the OTHER side, which the writer answers. (Counterclaim.)","✗ Ignoring who produced a statistic. → ✓ Name the source (the OECD, PISA) and judge its credibility. (Source.)","✗ Saying *18% of students are bad at maths*. → ✓ 18% lacked basic FINANCIAL literacy. (Read numbers precisely.)","✗ The OECD report that students… → ✓ The OECD reports that students… (Singular subject: **reports**.)","✗ Treating the concession as the writer changing their mind. → ✓ A concession admits a fair point, then the writer turns back. (Concede and counter.)","✗ Financial education are important. → ✓ Financial education is important. (Uncountable subject: **is**.)"],"arabic":"المقالة الجدلية تحاول إقناع القارئ بقبول ادعاء، ويُذكر الادعاء عادة في المقدمة أطروحةً مع لمحة عن الأسباب، وتطوّر كل فقرة عرض سببًا واحدًا تدعمه أدلة: إحصاءات وحقائق وأمثلة ومصادر خبيرة.\n\nوالكاتب القوي يتعامل مع الرأي الآخر: الادعاء المضاد رأي معارض، والرد يجيب عنه. ولاحظ الحركة: يعترف الكاتب بنقطة عادلة ثم ينعطف بـ However أو Yet أو Moreover ليبيّن لماذا يظل ادعاؤه قائمًا.\n\nالقارئ الفاحص يسأل: ما الادعاء؟ وما الأسباب؟ وأي دليل يدعم أي سبب؟ وهل المصدر مذكور وموثوق؟ وهل رُدّ على الادعاءات المضادة بإنصاف؟ ولاحظ الخاتمة التي تعيد الادعاء بكلمات جديدة وتنتهي بجملة لا تُنسى.\n\nانتبه: نسبة 18% تتعلق بالمعرفة المالية لا بالرياضيات، فاقرأ الأرقام بدقة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"You can solve equations – but can you spot a scam? Should every school teach money?","on_screen":"Financial Education Should Be Compulsory 📊","visual":"A student at a desk with an equation on one side and a fake prize message on a phone on the other."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"60% with a bank account or card. More than 85% shopping online. Teenagers are already consumers.","on_screen":"Reason 1: young people are already consumers","visual":"Animated percentages filling bars over teenagers holding phones."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"18% without basic financial literacy – and high performers are 72% more likely to save.","on_screen":"Reason 2: knowledge builds better habits","visual":"Two piggy banks, one filling faster than the other."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Isn't it the parents' job? Isn't the timetable full? Concede – then turn: However… Yet… Moreover…","on_screen":"Counterclaims and rebuttals","visual":"A debate scene where a speaker nods, then raises a finger and responds."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Not a luxury – a necessity. Read the claim, the reasons, the evidence and the answers.","on_screen":"Close reading an argument","visual":"The essay's structure appearing as building blocks."}],"captions_ar":["تستطيع حل المعادلات، لكن هل تستطيع كشف الاحتيال؟ هل يجب أن تعلّم كل مدرسة إدارة المال؟","60% لديهم حساب بنكي أو بطاقة، وأكثر من 85% يتسوقون عبر الإنترنت: المراهقون مستهلكون بالفعل.","18% بلا معرفة مالية أساسية، وأصحاب الأداء العالي أكثر ادخارًا بنسبة 72%.","أليست مهمة الوالدين؟ أليس الجدول ممتلئًا؟ اعترف ثم انعطف: However وYet وMoreover.","ليست رفاهية بل ضرورة: اقرأ الادعاء والأسباب والأدلة والردود."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, clear, persuasive female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Financial Education Should Be Compulsory","body":"Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations, explain photosynthesis and write a five-paragraph essay – but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget, compare two prices or recognize an online scam. For many young people around the world, this is not imaginary; it is reality. Money affects almost every decision adults make, yet in many schools financial education is optional or missing entirely. Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.\n\n**Young People Are Already Consumers** – Some people imagine that money is an adult problem. The evidence says otherwise. In 2022, the international PISA assessment tested the financial literacy of 15-year-olds in twenty countries and economies, including Saudi Arabia. According to the OECD, the organization that runs PISA, about 60% of the students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card, and more than 85% had bought something online in the previous twelve months. Teenagers today tap their phones to pay, download games that offer purchases inside the app and see advertisements every few minutes. Many also receive money as gifts, earn small amounts by helping in family businesses or manage their own pocket money for school meals. They are consumers now, not only in the future.\n\n**Knowledge Builds Better Habits** – The same assessment found that, on average across the OECD countries, 18% of students lacked basic financial literacy. In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems. This matters because knowledge and behavior are connected. The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something. Habits like these, once formed, can last a lifetime. A student who learns to compare prices at fifteen is likely to keep doing so at thirty-five, when the amounts involved are far larger.\n\n**Protecting Families and Communities** – Financial mistakes rarely stay private. A person who falls into debt may struggle to support a family, and a community full of people who cannot plan for emergencies is weaker when hard times arrive. Financial education also teaches young people to recognize scams and \"get rich quick\" schemes, which often target the people who know the least. Today, many of these scams arrive through text messages and social media, exactly where young people spend much of their time. In Islamic tradition, wealth is a trust, and paying zakat reminds believers that money carries a responsibility to others. Learning to manage money well is one way of honoring that responsibility.\n\n**Answering the Critics** – Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools. It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits. However, not every parent feels confident about money, and not every family talks about it openly. A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation, whatever their background. Other critics claim that the school timetable is already too crowded. Yet financial education does not need to replace other subjects; it can be combined with mathematics, for example by calculating savings goals or comparing the real cost of two phone plans. Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy.\n\n**A Lesson for Life** – Schools exist to prepare young people for the lives they will actually lead, and every one of those lives will involve earning, spending, saving and giving. Some of today's students will one day run businesses, manage family budgets or even advise governments. Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity. The sooner students learn to make smart choices about money, the stronger their futures – and their communities – will be.","word_count":658,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS2-1","genre":"opinion/argument","role":"main","difficulty":"900L–1050L","glossary":[{"word":"PISA","definition":"an international test of 15-year-olds organized by the OECD","arabic":"البرنامج الدولي لتقييم الطلبة"},{"word":"OECD","definition":"the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development, a group of countries that studies economies and education","arabic":"منظمة التعاون الاقتصادي والتنمية"},{"word":"scheme","definition":"a plan, often a dishonest one, to get money","arabic":"مخطط / خطة احتيالية"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the essay. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقالة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Which international assessment tested 15-year-olds' financial literacy in 2022?","answer":"PISA","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"بيزا.","options":["the SAT","IELTS","the STEP test","PISA"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"About what percentage of students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card?","answer":"about 60%","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"نحو 60%.","options":["about 18%","about 85%","about 60%","about 72%"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What percentage of students lacked basic financial literacy, on average across the OECD countries?","answer":"18%","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"18%.","options":["18%","50%","60%","85%"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How much more likely were high performers to compare prices in different shops?","answer":"50% more likely","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"50%.","options":["50% more likely","72% more likely","18% more likely","twice as likely"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"According to the essay, who do scams and *get rich quick* schemes often target?","answer":"the people who know the least","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"الأقل معرفة.","options":["bank managers","the people who know the least","teachers","only adults over sixty"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which subject does the writer suggest combining financial education with?","answer":"mathematics","explanation":"Paragraph 5.","explanation_ar":"الرياضيات.","options":["art","mathematics","physical education","geography"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Claim, evidence, rebuttal and SAT-style questions. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الادعاء والدليل والرد وأسئلة SAT: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What is the writer's CLAIM?","answer":"Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["Money affects many decisions.","Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject.","PISA tests 15-year-olds.","Parents should teach their children about money."],"dok":1,"skill":"claim","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which reason does the statistic *more than 85% had bought something online* support?","answer":"Young people are already consumers.","explanation":"Evidence and reasons.","explanation_ar":"المراهقون مستهلكون.","options":["Young people are already consumers.","Financial knowledge protects communities.","The timetable is too crowded.","Zakat is a responsibility."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a CONCESSION?","answer":"It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits.","explanation":"Concession.","explanation_ar":"اعتراف بنقطة عادلة.","options":["However, not every parent feels confident about money.","A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation.","It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits.","Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks tend to perform better."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does the writer respond to the claim that the timetable is too crowded?","answer":"by suggesting that financial education can be combined with mathematics","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"دمجها مع الرياضيات.","options":["by agreeing that it is impossible","by proposing a longer school day","by suggesting that financial education can be combined with mathematics","by removing science from the timetable"],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** Which choice BEST describes the function of the sentence *In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems*?","answer":"It explains the meaning of a statistic in simpler terms.","explanation":"Function of a sentence.","explanation_ar":"يشرح معنى الإحصائية.","options":["It introduces a counterclaim.","It gives the essay's thesis.","It describes a personal experience.","It explains the meaning of a statistic in simpler terms."],"dok":3,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** Which finding, if true, would MOST directly weaken the writer's second reason?","answer":"Students who score high in financial literacy save no more often than students who score low.","explanation":"Command of evidence.","explanation_ar":"يضعف السبب الثاني.","options":["Many teenagers own smartphones.","Some parents teach their children to save.","PISA tests reading as well as mathematics.","Students who score high in financial literacy save no more often than students who score low."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the thesis statement in the introduction.","answer":"Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"الأطروحة.","evidence":"Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS2-1","dok":1,"skill":"claim","stage":"freer","sentences":["Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations, explain photosynthesis and write a five-paragraph essay – but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget, compare two prices or recognize an online scam.","For many young people around the world, this is not imaginary; it is reality.","Money affects almost every decision adults make, yet in many schools financial education is optional or missing entirely.","Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.","**Young People Are Already Consumers** – Some people imagine that money is an adult problem.","The evidence says otherwise.","In 2022, the international PISA assessment tested the financial literacy of 15-year-olds in twenty countries and economies, including Saudi Arabia.","According to the OECD, the organization that runs PISA, about 60% of the students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card, and more than 85% had bought something online in the previous twelve months.","Teenagers today tap their phones to pay, download games that offer purchases inside the app and see advertisements every few minutes.","Many also receive money as gifts, earn small amounts by helping in family businesses or manage their own pocket money for school meals.","They are consumers now, not only in the future.","**Knowledge Builds Better Habits** – The same assessment found that, on average across the OECD countries, 18% of students lacked basic financial literacy.","In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems.","This matters because knowledge and behavior are connected.","The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something.","Habits like these, once formed, can last a lifetime.","A student who learns to compare prices at fifteen is likely to keep doing so at thirty-five, when the amounts involved are far larger.","**Protecting Families and Communities** – Financial mistakes rarely stay private.","A person who falls into debt may struggle to support a family, and a community full of people who cannot plan for emergencies is weaker when hard times arrive.","Financial education also teaches young people to recognize scams and \"get rich quick\" schemes, which often target the people who know the least.","Today, many of these scams arrive through text messages and social media, exactly where young people spend much of their time.","In Islamic tradition, wealth is a trust, and paying zakat reminds believers that money carries a responsibility to others.","Learning to manage money well is one way of honoring that responsibility.","**Answering the Critics** – Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools.","It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits.","However, not every parent feels confident about money, and not every family talks about it openly.","A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation, whatever their background.","Other critics claim that the school timetable is already too crowded.","Yet financial education does not need to replace other subjects; it can be combined with mathematics, for example by calculating savings goals or comparing the real cost of two phone plans.","Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy.","**A Lesson for Life** – Schools exist to prepare young people for the lives they will actually lead, and every one of those lives will involve earning, spending, saving and giving.","Some of today's students will one day run businesses, manage family budgets or even advise governments.","Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity.","The sooner students learn to make smart choices about money, the stronger their futures – and their communities – will be."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that connects financial literacy to saving behavior.","answer":"The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something.","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الربط بالسلوك.","evidence":"The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations, explain photosynthesis and write a five-paragraph essay – but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget, compare two prices or recognize an online scam.","For many young people around the world, this is not imaginary; it is reality.","Money affects almost every decision adults make, yet in many schools financial education is optional or missing entirely.","Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.","**Young People Are Already Consumers** – Some people imagine that money is an adult problem.","The evidence says otherwise.","In 2022, the international PISA assessment tested the financial literacy of 15-year-olds in twenty countries and economies, including Saudi Arabia.","According to the OECD, the organization that runs PISA, about 60% of the students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card, and more than 85% had bought something online in the previous twelve months.","Teenagers today tap their phones to pay, download games that offer purchases inside the app and see advertisements every few minutes.","Many also receive money as gifts, earn small amounts by helping in family businesses or manage their own pocket money for school meals.","They are consumers now, not only in the future.","**Knowledge Builds Better Habits** – The same assessment found that, on average across the OECD countries, 18% of students lacked basic financial literacy.","In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems.","This matters because knowledge and behavior are connected.","The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something.","Habits like these, once formed, can last a lifetime.","A student who learns to compare prices at fifteen is likely to keep doing so at thirty-five, when the amounts involved are far larger.","**Protecting Families and Communities** – Financial mistakes rarely stay private.","A person who falls into debt may struggle to support a family, and a community full of people who cannot plan for emergencies is weaker when hard times arrive.","Financial education also teaches young people to recognize scams and \"get rich quick\" schemes, which often target the people who know the least.","Today, many of these scams arrive through text messages and social media, exactly where young people spend much of their time.","In Islamic tradition, wealth is a trust, and paying zakat reminds believers that money carries a responsibility to others.","Learning to manage money well is one way of honoring that responsibility.","**Answering the Critics** – Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools.","It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits.","However, not every parent feels confident about money, and not every family talks about it openly.","A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation, whatever their background.","Other critics claim that the school timetable is already too crowded.","Yet financial education does not need to replace other subjects; it can be combined with mathematics, for example by calculating savings goals or comparing the real cost of two phone plans.","Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy.","**A Lesson for Life** – Schools exist to prepare young people for the lives they will actually lead, and every one of those lives will involve earning, spending, saving and giving.","Some of today's students will one day run businesses, manage family budgets or even advise governments.","Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity.","The sooner students learn to make smart choices about money, the stronger their futures – and their communities – will be."]},{"q":"What does *compulsory* mean in *make financial education a compulsory subject*?","answer":"required for all students","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"إلزامي.","options":["required for all students","chosen by a few students","taught only at university","taught only at home"],"dok":2,"skill":"words in context","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention zakat in paragraph 4?","answer":"to connect managing money well with the responsibility that wealth carries","explanation":"Author's purpose.","explanation_ar":"ربط إدارة المال بالمسؤولية.","options":["to explain how to calculate taxes","to connect managing money well with the responsibility that wealth carries","to argue that zakat should be taught instead of mathematics","to criticize families who do not save"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Analyze the argument.","instructions_ar":"حلّل الحجة.","items":[{"q":"State the writer's three reasons in your own words.","answer":"1) Teenagers already use money and buy things online. 2) Learning about money leads to better habits like saving. 3) Money knowledge protects families and communities from debt and scams.","explanation":"Reasons.","explanation_ar":"الأسباب.","dok":2,"skill":"claim","stage":"production"},{"q":"Choose one statistic and explain why it is convincing evidence.","answer":"The 72% statistic is convincing because it comes from the OECD, a credible international organization, and it directly links knowledge with a good habit (saving).","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":3,"skill":"evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain how the writer answers the counterclaim that money is the parents' job.","answer":"The writer agrees that families are important but argues that not all parents feel confident or talk about money, so a compulsory course gives every student the same foundation.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرد.","dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"production"},{"q":"Do you agree with the writer? Give one reason from the essay and one from your own experience.","answer":"I agree. The essay shows that most teenagers already buy things online, and I have seen friends spend all their money on in-game purchases without planning.","explanation":"Respond.","explanation_ar":"استجب.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Financial Education Should Be Compulsory\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Financial Education Should Be Compulsory”.","answer":"658 words","title":"Financial Education Should Be Compulsory","body":"Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations, explain photosynthesis and write a five-paragraph essay – but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget, compare two prices or recognize an online scam. For many young people around the world, this is not imaginary; it is reality. Money affects almost every decision adults make, yet in many schools financial education is optional or missing entirely. Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities.\n\n**Young People Are Already Consumers** – Some people imagine that money is an adult problem. The evidence says otherwise. In 2022, the international PISA assessment tested the financial literacy of 15-year-olds in twenty countries and economies, including Saudi Arabia. According to the OECD, the organization that runs PISA, about 60% of the students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card, and more than 85% had bought something online in the previous twelve months. Teenagers today tap their phones to pay, download games that offer purchases inside the app and see advertisements every few minutes. Many also receive money as gifts, earn small amounts by helping in family businesses or manage their own pocket money for school meals. They are consumers now, not only in the future.\n\n**Knowledge Builds Better Habits** – The same assessment found that, on average across the OECD countries, 18% of students lacked basic financial literacy. In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems. This matters because knowledge and behavior are connected. The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something. Habits like these, once formed, can last a lifetime. A student who learns to compare prices at fifteen is likely to keep doing so at thirty-five, when the amounts involved are far larger.\n\n**Protecting Families and Communities** – Financial mistakes rarely stay private. A person who falls into debt may struggle to support a family, and a community full of people who cannot plan for emergencies is weaker when hard times arrive. Financial education also teaches young people to recognize scams and \"get rich quick\" schemes, which often target the people who know the least. Today, many of these scams arrive through text messages and social media, exactly where young people spend much of their time. In Islamic tradition, wealth is a trust, and paying zakat reminds believers that money carries a responsibility to others. Learning to manage money well is one way of honoring that responsibility.\n\n**Answering the Critics** – Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools. It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits. However, not every parent feels confident about money, and not every family talks about it openly. A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation, whatever their background. Other critics claim that the school timetable is already too crowded. Yet financial education does not need to replace other subjects; it can be combined with mathematics, for example by calculating savings goals or comparing the real cost of two phone plans. Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy.\n\n**A Lesson for Life** – Schools exist to prepare young people for the lives they will actually lead, and every one of those lives will involve earning, spending, saving and giving. Some of today's students will one day run businesses, manage family budgets or even advise governments. Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity. The sooner students learn to make smart choices about money, the stronger their futures – and their communities – will be.","words":658,"limit":287}],"time_limit_sec":287},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Argument Map","instructions":"Organizer: Write the claim, each reason with its evidence, the counterclaims with the writer's rebuttals, and the conclusion's final message.","items":[{"q":"Claim (thesis)","answer":"Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence","answer":"Young people are already consumers: about 60% have a bank account or card; more than 85% bought something online (OECD, PISA 2022)."},{"q":"Reason 2 + evidence","answer":"Knowledge builds habits: 18% lack basic financial literacy; high performers are 72% more likely to save (OECD)."},{"q":"Reason 3 + evidence","answer":"It protects families and communities: avoiding debt, recognizing scams, honoring the responsibility of wealth and zakat."},{"q":"Counterclaims and rebuttals","answer":"Parents' job → not every parent feels confident; a course gives everyone the same foundation. Crowded timetable → combine it with mathematics; school tasks improve performance."},{"q":"Conclusion – final message","answer":"Financial education is not a luxury but a necessity; it strengthens futures and communities."}]}],"game":{"name":"Argument Architects","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Argument builder: sentence blocks fall from the sky; the child places each one on the correct floor of the building. A complete, correct building lights up; a misplaced block makes the building wobble.","Teams receive mixed sentence strips.","They place each strip on the correct part of the building.","They must match each piece of evidence to its reason.","Correct placement = 1 point; correct evidence match = 1 bonus point.","The first team with a stable building explains its structure to the class."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *claim* in an argument essay?","answer":"the writer's main position that they want readers to accept","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["a fact about money","a question at the end","a list of sources","the writer's main position that they want readers to accept"],"dok":1,"skill":"claim"},{"q":"What does *optional* mean?","answer":"that you can choose to do or not do","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"اختياري.","options":["required by law","that you can choose to do or not do","very expensive","forbidden"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The message promising a free phone was a **scam**.* *Scam* means ___.","answer":"a dishonest trick to get money","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"احتيال.","options":["a school subject","a dishonest trick to get money","a type of bank account","a real prize"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which organization runs the PISA assessment?","answer":"the OECD","explanation":"Key detail.","explanation_ar":"OECD.","options":["the World Health Organization","the Ministry of Commerce","a mobile phone company","the OECD"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"Which sentence uses the verb correctly?","answer":"The OECD reports that many students save money.","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"reports.","options":["The OECD report that many students save money.","The OECD reporting that many students save money.","The OECD reports that many students save money.","The OECD are reports that many students save money."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which word signals the writer's TURN from a concession to a rebuttal?","answer":"However","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"However.","options":["However","Imagine","Including","Because"],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"Why does the writer name the OECD as the source of the statistics?","answer":"to show that the evidence comes from a credible organization","explanation":"Credibility.","explanation_ar":"مصداقية المصدر.","options":["to make the essay longer","to criticize the OECD","to show that the evidence comes from a credible organization","to introduce a counterclaim"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence"},{"q":"What is the effect of the final line *not a luxury; it is a necessity*?","answer":"It restates the claim memorably by contrasting two key words.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"إعادة الادعاء بأسلوب لا يُنسى.","options":["It introduces a new reason.","It shows that the writer has changed their mind.","It restates the claim memorably by contrasting two key words.","It gives a new statistic."],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** Which statement about the evidence in the essay is BEST supported?","answer":"The writer uses statistics from an international assessment to show that teenagers already make financial decisions.","explanation":"Command of evidence.","explanation_ar":"إحصاءات تقييم دولي.","options":["The writer uses statistics from an international assessment to show that teenagers already make financial decisions.","The writer uses only personal stories as evidence.","The writer provides no evidence for any reason.","The writer's evidence comes from a single student's survey."],"dok":3,"skill":"evidence"},{"q":"How does the essay help answer *How can smart choices about money shape our future?*","answer":"It argues that learning about money early builds habits that protect individuals and communities for life.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"عادات تحمي مدى الحياة.","options":["It argues that money does not matter.","It argues that learning about money early builds habits that protect individuals and communities for life.","It says that only adults make money choices.","It describes how to win prizes online."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'compulsory': a timetable with one subject marked REQUIRED"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'optional': a menu with a choice of two items"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'literacy': a student reading a simple money chart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'scam': a phone screen with a fake prize message and a warning sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'assessment': students taking a test at computers"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'timetable': a weekly school schedule on a wall"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'critic': a person shaking their head while reading a proposal"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-01","prompt":"A student who can solve equations but may miss a scam."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-02","prompt":"Teenagers as everyday consumers."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS2-03","prompt":"A concession and rebuttal in a debate."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V01","script":"compulsory. English is a compulsory subject at our school."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V02","script":"optional. The art club is optional."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V03","script":"literacy. Financial literacy helps people make smart choices."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V04","script":"scam. The message promising a free phone was a scam."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V05","script":"assessment. The PISA assessment tests 15-year-olds."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V06","script":"timetable. The school timetable is already full."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-V07","script":"critic. Critics say the course would take time from other subjects."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-T1","script":"Imagine finishing school able to solve complex equations, explain photosynthesis and write a five-paragraph essay – but not knowing how to plan a monthly budget, compare two prices or recognize an online scam. For many young people around the world, this is not imaginary; it is reality. Money affects almost every decision adults make, yet in many schools financial education is optional or missing entirely. Every secondary school should make financial education a compulsory subject, because young people are already making financial decisions, because good habits are easier to build early and because financial knowledge protects both individuals and communities. **Young People Are Already Consumers** – Some people imagine that money is an adult problem. The evidence says otherwise. In 2022, the international PISA assessment tested the financial literacy of 15-year-olds in twenty countries and economies, including Saudi Arabia. According to the OECD, the organization that runs PISA, about 60% of the students in the participating OECD countries had a bank account or a payment card, and more than 85% had bought something online in the previous twelve months. Teenagers today tap their phones to pay, download games that offer purchases inside the app and see advertisements every few minutes. Many also receive money as gifts, earn small amounts by helping in family businesses or manage their own pocket money for school meals. They are consumers now, not only in the future. **Knowledge Builds Better Habits** – The same assessment found that, on average across the OECD countries, 18% of students lacked basic financial literacy. In other words, nearly one in five could not apply what they knew to simple real-life money problems. This matters because knowledge and behavior are connected. The OECD reports that high performers in financial literacy were 72% more likely than low performers to save money and 50% more likely to compare prices in different shops before buying something. Habits like these, once formed, can last a lifetime. A student who learns to compare prices at fifteen is likely to keep doing so at thirty-five, when the amounts involved are far larger. **Protecting Families and Communities** – Financial mistakes rarely stay private. A person who falls into debt may struggle to support a family, and a community full of people who cannot plan for emergencies is weaker when hard times arrive. Financial education also teaches young people to recognize scams and \"get rich quick\" schemes, which often target the people who know the least. Today, many of these scams arrive through text messages and social media, exactly where young people spend much of their time. In Islamic tradition, wealth is a trust, and paying zakat reminds believers that money carries a responsibility to others. Learning to manage money well is one way of honoring that responsibility. **Answering the Critics** – Some argue that teaching money matters is the job of parents, not schools. It is true that families play an important role, and many parents teach their children excellent habits. However, not every parent feels confident about money, and not every family talks about it openly. A compulsory course would give every student the same foundation, whatever their background. Other critics claim that the school timetable is already too crowded. Yet financial education does not need to replace other subjects; it can be combined with mathematics, for example by calculating savings goals or comparing the real cost of two phone plans. Moreover, the OECD notes that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy. **A Lesson for Life** – Schools exist to prepare young people for the lives they will actually lead, and every one of those lives will involve earning, spending, saving and giving. Some of today's students will one day run businesses, manage family budgets or even advise governments. Making financial education compulsory is not a luxury; it is a necessity. The sooner students learn to make smart choices about money, the stronger their futures – and their communities – will be."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS2-VID","script":"You can solve equations – but can you spot a scam? Should every school teach money? 60% with a bank account or card. More than 85% shopping online. Teenagers are already consumers. 18% without basic financial literacy – and high performers are 72% more likely to save. Isn't it the parents' job? Isn't the timetable full? Concede – then turn: However… Yet… Moreover… Not a luxury – a necessity. Read the claim, the reasons, the evidence and the answers."}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3","title":"Evaluating claims and evidence: \"How to Build Your First Savings Plan\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["follow and explain the steps of a procedural text about building a savings plan","evaluate claims by asking whether the evidence is relevant, sufficient and credible, and recognize weak reasoning such as hasty generalizations","compare the evidence in *Financial Education Should Be Compulsory* and *How to Build Your First Savings Plan*"],"objectives_ar":["اتباع خطوات نص إجرائي عن بناء خطة ادخار وشرحها.","تقويم الادعاءات بالسؤال عن ملاءمة الدليل وكفايته ومصداقيته، والتعرّف إلى الاستدلال الضعيف كالتعميم المتسرّع.","مقارنة الأدلة في النصين."],"success_criteria":["I can explain each step of the savings plan and why it helps.","I can test a claim with the three questions: relevant? sufficient? credible?","I can spot an anecdote or a hasty generalization used as weak evidence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع شرح كل خطوة في خطة الادخار وفائدتها.","أستطيع اختبار الادعاء بثلاثة أسئلة: هل الدليل ملائم؟ كافٍ؟ موثوق؟","أستطيع اكتشاف الحكاية الفردية أو التعميم المتسرّع دليلًا ضعيفًا."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"allowance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈlaʊəns/","definition":"money that parents give their children regularly","arabic":"مصروف الجيب","example":"He saves half of his weekly allowance.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"track","pos":"verb","ipa":"/træk/","definition":"to follow and record something over time","arabic":"يتتبّع / يرصد","example":"Track your spending for two weeks.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"deadline","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɛdˌlaɪn/","definition":"the time or date by which something must be done","arabic":"موعد نهائي","example":"My deadline is the summer holiday.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"vague","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/veɪɡ/","definition":"not clear or not detailed","arabic":"مبهم / غامض","example":"*Save money* is a vague goal.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"impulse","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪmˌpʌls/","definition":"a sudden wish to do something without thinking","arabic":"اندفاع / نزوة","example":"He bought the game on impulse.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"anecdote","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænɪkˌdoʊt/","definition":"a short story about one person's experience","arabic":"حكاية / قصة شخصية","example":"One anecdote is not enough to prove a claim.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"hasty generalization","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈheɪsti ˌdʒɛnərəlɪˈzeɪʃən/","definition":"a conclusion about a whole group based on too few examples","arabic":"تعميم متسرّع","example":"*My cousin lost money, so all saving is useless* is a hasty generalization.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Every text makes **claims** – statements that the writer wants you to believe. A careful reader **tests** each claim with three questions. **Relevant?** Does the evidence actually connect to this claim? **Sufficient?** Is there enough evidence – more than one example? **Credible?** Does it come from a trustworthy, named source, or from calculation you can check?\n\nDifferent kinds of evidence have different strengths. **Statistics from a credible organization** (the OECD) are strong for claims about large groups. **Calculations** can be verified (*10 riyals × 52 weeks = 520 riyals*). **Expert advice** is useful when the expert is named. An **anecdote** (*my cousin saved enough for a car*) can make a point memorable, but one story is **not sufficient** to prove a general claim.\n\nWatch for **weak reasoning**. A **hasty generalization** draws a big conclusion from too few examples (*My friend never saves, so teenagers can't save*). A **too-good-to-be-true promise** (*double your money in a week!*) gives no evidence at all. In SAT-style questions, choose the evidence that **most directly** supports or weakens the claim – not the evidence that is merely on the same topic.","examples":["Claim + verifiable calculation: ***Small amounts add up: if you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year.***","Claim + reason: ***A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to, because you can measure your progress.***","Claim + credible statistic (LS2): ***According to the OECD, … about 60% of the students … had a bank account or a payment card.***","Relevant? ✓ *60% have a bank account* → supports *young people are already consumers*.","Relevant? ✗ *Many teenagers like football* → does NOT support *financial education should be compulsory*.","Sufficient? ✗ *One student in my class saved 1,000 riyals, so saving is easy for everyone.*","Anecdote used well: ***Many people are surprised to discover how much they spend on small things.*** (a common experience, not proof)","Hasty generalization: ✗ *I tried saving for one week and failed, so savings plans never work.*","No evidence at all: ✗ *Send us 100 riyals and we will double it tomorrow!*"],"common_errors":["✗ Believing a claim because it is written confidently. → ✓ Ask: relevant? sufficient? credible? (Test claims.)","✗ Using one story as proof for everyone. → ✓ Add statistics or several examples. (Sufficient evidence.)","✗ Choosing evidence that is only on the same topic. → ✓ Choose evidence that directly supports THIS claim. (Relevant evidence.)","✗ Trusting a source with no name. → ✓ Prefer named, expert or official sources. (Credible evidence.)","✗ Save money on impulse. → ✓ Spend money on impulse. / Save money with a plan. (*on impulse* = without thinking.)","✗ If you will save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals. → ✓ If you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals. (First conditional: **if + present**.)","✗ The evidence are strong. → ✓ The evidence is strong. (*evidence* is uncountable.)"],"arabic":"كل نص يقدّم ادعاءات يريد الكاتب أن تصدّقها، والقارئ الحريص يختبر كل ادعاء بثلاثة أسئلة: هل الدليل ملائم ومرتبط بهذا الادعاء فعلًا؟ وهل هو كافٍ؟ وهل هو موثوق من مصدر معروف أو حساب يمكن التحقق منه؟\n\nلأنواع الأدلة قوة مختلفة: الإحصاءات من منظمة موثوقة قوية في الادعاءات عن المجموعات الكبيرة، والحسابات يمكن التحقق منها، ورأي الخبير مفيد إذا ذُكر اسمه، أما الحكاية الفردية فتجعل الفكرة لا تُنسى لكنها لا تكفي لإثبات ادعاء عام.\n\nاحذر الاستدلال الضعيف: التعميم المتسرّع يبني نتيجة كبيرة على أمثلة قليلة، والوعد الذي يبدو أجمل من أن يكون حقيقيًا لا يقدّم دليلًا. وفي أسئلة SAT اختر الدليل الذي يدعم الادعاء أو يضعفه مباشرة.\n\nانتبه: evidence اسم غير معدود، والجملة الشرطية الأولى: if + مضارع."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:40","narration":"Ten riyals a week. Fifty-two weeks. Five hundred and twenty riyals. Small amounts add up!","on_screen":"How to Build Your First Savings Plan 🐷","visual":"Coins stacking week by week into a tall pile."},{"time":"00:40–01:40","narration":"Set a goal. Track. Needs before wants. Pay yourself first. Keep it safe. Review.","on_screen":"Six steps","visual":"Six stepping stones leading to a bicycle."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Is the evidence relevant? Is it sufficient? Is it credible?","on_screen":"Three questions for every claim","visual":"A magnifying glass checking three labels on a claim card."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"'My cousin failed, so nobody can save!' – that's a hasty generalization.","on_screen":"Weak reasoning","visual":"One small figure pointing at a huge crowd, with a red cross."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Double your money in a week? If it sounds too good to be true, it probably is.","on_screen":"Spot the scam","visual":"A flashy offer dissolving into smoke."}],"captions_ar":["عشرة ريالات في الأسبوع، واثنان وخمسون أسبوعًا: خمسمائة وعشرون ريالًا. المبالغ الصغيرة تتراكم!","حدّد هدفًا، وتتبّع، وقدّم الحاجات على الرغبات، وادّخر أولًا، واحفظ مالك، وراجع.","هل الدليل ملائم؟ هل هو كافٍ؟ هل هو موثوق؟","«ابن عمي فشل، إذن لا أحد يستطيع الادخار!» هذا تعميم متسرّع.","تضاعف مالك في أسبوع؟ إن بدا أجمل من أن يكون حقيقيًا فهو على الأرجح ليس كذلك."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, practical, encouraging male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How to Build Your First Savings Plan","body":"Saving money is not only for adults with large salaries. Anyone with a small income – a weekly allowance, Eid gifts or money from a summer job – can learn to save. A savings plan turns good intentions into a simple routine. Follow these six steps.\n\n**You will need:** a notebook or a simple budgeting app, a safe place to keep your savings and about fifteen minutes each week.\n\n**Step 1 – Set a clear goal.** Decide what you are saving for and how much it costs. *Save money* is vague; *Save 600 riyals for a bicycle by the summer holiday* is clear. A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to, because you can measure your progress.\n\n**Step 2 – Track your income and spending.** For two weeks, write down every riyal that comes in and every riyal that goes out. Many people are surprised to discover how much they spend on small things, such as snacks, mobile data and in-app purchases.\n\n**Step 3 – Separate needs from wants.** Look at your list and mark each expense N (need) or W (want). Needs, such as school supplies, come first. Wants are not forbidden, but they are the easiest place to cut back.\n\n**Step 4 – Pay yourself first.** As soon as you receive money, put your savings aside before you spend anything. Small amounts add up: if you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year.\n\n**Step 5 – Keep your savings safe.** Use a locked box at home or, with a parent's help, a youth bank account. Keeping savings out of easy reach makes it harder to spend them on impulse.\n\n**Step 6 – Review and reward.** Once a month, compare your progress with your goal. If you are behind, find one expense to reduce or extend your deadline a little. When you reach your goal, celebrate – and then set a new one.\n\n**Remember to share.** Many families include charity (*sadaqah*) in their plans. In Islam, a person whose savings stay above a minimum amount, called the *nisab*, for a full lunar year pays zakat of 2.5%. Giving is part of a balanced plan, not a break from it.\n\n**Warning:** Be careful of anyone who promises to double your savings quickly. If an offer sounds too good to be true, it probably is. Always ask a trusted adult before you put your money anywhere new.","word_count":397,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS3-1","genre":"procedural","role":"paired","difficulty":"900L–1050L","glossary":[{"word":"allowance","definition":"money that parents give children regularly","arabic":"مصروف الجيب"},{"word":"on impulse","definition":"suddenly, without thinking or planning","arabic":"باندفاع / دون تفكير"},{"word":"nisab","definition":"the minimum amount of wealth on which zakat must be paid","arabic":"النصاب"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the procedure. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص الإجرائي واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Which goal does the text describe as CLEAR?","answer":"Save 600 riyals for a bicycle by the summer holiday.","explanation":"Step 1.","explanation_ar":"هدف واضح.","options":["Save money.","Spend less sometimes.","Be rich one day.","Save 600 riyals for a bicycle by the summer holiday."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"For how long should you track your income and spending in Step 2?","answer":"for two weeks","explanation":"Step 2.","explanation_ar":"أسبوعين.","options":["for one day","for one year","for two weeks","for six months"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In Step 3, how do you mark each expense?","answer":"N for need or W for want","explanation":"Step 3.","explanation_ar":"N أو W.","options":["N for need or W for want","G for good or B for bad","with a price in red","with a smiley face"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does *pay yourself first* mean?","answer":"Put your savings aside before you spend anything.","explanation":"Step 4.","explanation_ar":"ادّخر قبل أن تنفق.","options":["Put your savings aside before you spend anything.","Buy something for yourself before saving.","Ask your parents to pay you.","Pay your friends back first."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How often should you review your progress, according to Step 6?","answer":"once a month","explanation":"Step 6.","explanation_ar":"مرة في الشهر.","options":["every day","once a month","once a year","never"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What should you do before you put your money anywhere new?","answer":"ask a trusted adult","explanation":"Warning.","explanation_ar":"اسأل شخصًا بالغًا موثوقًا.","options":["double it first","spend half of it","tell everyone online","ask a trusted adult"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Evaluate claims and SAT-style questions. Choose.","instructions_ar":"قيّم الادعاءات وأسئلة SAT: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which evidence supports the claim *Small amounts add up*?","answer":"If you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year.","explanation":"Relevant evidence.","explanation_ar":"الحساب.","options":["Wants are not forbidden.","If you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year.","Use a locked box at home.","Many families include charity in their plans."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why is the evidence for *Small amounts add up* CREDIBLE?","answer":"because readers can check the calculation themselves","explanation":"Credible evidence.","explanation_ar":"يمكن التحقق منه.","options":["because a famous person said it","because it is in bold type","because it is a personal story","because readers can check the calculation themselves"],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which statement is a HASTY GENERALIZATION?","answer":"My brother bought a game on impulse, so all teenagers waste their money.","explanation":"Hasty generalization.","explanation_ar":"تعميم متسرّع.","options":["Tracking spending helps many people notice small expenses.","My brother bought a game on impulse, so all teenagers waste their money.","Saving 10 riyals a week gives 520 riyals a year.","Needs should come before wants."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How is the evidence in the procedure DIFFERENT from the evidence in the essay?","answer":"The procedure uses reasons and simple calculations; the essay uses statistics from an international organization.","explanation":"Comparing texts.","explanation_ar":"حسابات مقابل إحصاءات.","options":["The procedure uses reasons and simple calculations; the essay uses statistics from an international organization.","Both use only anecdotes.","The procedure uses more statistics than the essay.","The essay uses no evidence."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** A student claims that tracking spending helps people save. Which finding, if true, would MOST directly SUPPORT this claim?","answer":"People who recorded their expenses for a month saved more, on average, than similar people who did not.","explanation":"Command of evidence.","explanation_ar":"يدعم الادعاء مباشرة.","options":["Many people own notebooks.","Some apps are free to download.","People who recorded their expenses for a month saved more, on average, than similar people who did not.","Snacks are cheaper in supermarkets than in cafés."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** Which choice BEST describes the function of the *Warning* at the end of the text?","answer":"It cautions readers against offers that promise quick returns without evidence.","explanation":"Text structure.","explanation_ar":"تحذير من العروض المريبة.","options":["It introduces the first step of the plan.","It gives a statistic about saving.","It cautions readers against offers that promise quick returns without evidence.","It argues that saving is unnecessary."],"dok":3,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Claim Tester.","instructions_ar":"أكمل أداة اختبار الادعاءات.","items":[{"q":"Test the claim *A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to.* Is the evidence relevant, sufficient and credible?","answer":"Relevant: yes – the reason (you can measure progress) connects to the claim. Sufficient: partly – it is a logical reason but has no data. Credible: it makes sense, but a study would make it stronger.","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one claim from the essay and test its evidence.","answer":"Claim: young people are already consumers. Evidence: OECD data – about 60% have a bank account or card and more than 85% bought something online. Relevant, sufficient and credible.","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"freer"},{"q":"What does *vague* mean in *Save money is vague*?","answer":"not clear or detailed","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"مبهم.","options":["not clear or detailed","very exciting","completely wrong","too expensive"],"dok":2,"skill":"words in context","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which phrase from the text means *suddenly, without thinking*?","answer":"on impulse","explanation":"Words in context.","explanation_ar":"باندفاع.","options":["out of easy reach","pay yourself first","a balanced plan","on impulse"],"dok":2,"skill":"words in context","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Evaluate and connect.","instructions_ar":"قيّم واربط.","items":[{"q":"Explain which step you think is most important and justify your choice.","answer":"Step 4 is the most important because saving before spending makes saving automatic; if you wait until the end of the week, there is often nothing left.","explanation":"Justify.","explanation_ar":"برّر.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"production"},{"q":"Rewrite this weak claim so that it has relevant and sufficient evidence: *Saving is easy because my friend did it.*","answer":"Saving can be manageable: saving just 10 riyals a week gives 520 riyals a year, and the OECD found that students with strong financial literacy are much more likely to save.","explanation":"Strengthen.","explanation_ar":"قوِّ الادعاء.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"production"},{"q":"How does the procedure provide practical support for the essay's claim?","answer":"The essay argues that students need financial education; the procedure shows the kind of practical skills, like goal-setting and tracking, that such a course could teach.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"اربط.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain why *double your savings quickly* is a warning sign.","answer":"It promises a large reward with no evidence and no explanation; real saving grows slowly, so such offers are often scams.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"How to Build Your First Savings Plan\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “How to Build Your First Savings Plan”.","answer":"397 words","title":"How to Build Your First Savings Plan","body":"Saving money is not only for adults with large salaries. Anyone with a small income – a weekly allowance, Eid gifts or money from a summer job – can learn to save. A savings plan turns good intentions into a simple routine. Follow these six steps.\n\n**You will need:** a notebook or a simple budgeting app, a safe place to keep your savings and about fifteen minutes each week.\n\n**Step 1 – Set a clear goal.** Decide what you are saving for and how much it costs. *Save money* is vague; *Save 600 riyals for a bicycle by the summer holiday* is clear. A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to, because you can measure your progress.\n\n**Step 2 – Track your income and spending.** For two weeks, write down every riyal that comes in and every riyal that goes out. Many people are surprised to discover how much they spend on small things, such as snacks, mobile data and in-app purchases.\n\n**Step 3 – Separate needs from wants.** Look at your list and mark each expense N (need) or W (want). Needs, such as school supplies, come first. Wants are not forbidden, but they are the easiest place to cut back.\n\n**Step 4 – Pay yourself first.** As soon as you receive money, put your savings aside before you spend anything. Small amounts add up: if you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year.\n\n**Step 5 – Keep your savings safe.** Use a locked box at home or, with a parent's help, a youth bank account. Keeping savings out of easy reach makes it harder to spend them on impulse.\n\n**Step 6 – Review and reward.** Once a month, compare your progress with your goal. If you are behind, find one expense to reduce or extend your deadline a little. When you reach your goal, celebrate – and then set a new one.\n\n**Remember to share.** Many families include charity (*sadaqah*) in their plans. In Islam, a person whose savings stay above a minimum amount, called the *nisab*, for a full lunar year pays zakat of 2.5%. Giving is part of a balanced plan, not a break from it.\n\n**Warning:** Be careful of anyone who promises to double your savings quickly. If an offer sounds too good to be true, it probably is. Always ask a trusted adult before you put your money anywhere new.","words":397,"limit":179}],"time_limit_sec":179},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Claim Tester","instructions":"Organizer: Choose claims from both texts. For each, write the evidence and test it: relevant, sufficient, credible. Then give a verdict.","items":[{"q":"Claim 1 (procedure, Step 4)","answer":"Small amounts add up."},{"q":"Evidence and test","answer":"10 riyals × 52 weeks = 520 riyals: relevant ✓, sufficient ✓ (a full calculation), credible ✓ (anyone can check it)."},{"q":"Claim 2 (procedure, Step 1)","answer":"A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to."},{"q":"Evidence and test","answer":"Reason: you can measure your progress. Relevant ✓; sufficient – partly (a logical reason, no statistics); credible – common sense."},{"q":"Claim 3 (essay, Reason 2)","answer":"Knowledge builds better habits."},{"q":"Verdicts – which claim is best supported, and why?","answer":"OECD: high performers 72% more likely to save. Relevant ✓, sufficient ✓ (large international study), credible ✓ (named organization)."}]}],"game":{"name":"Claim Court","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Claim court: a claim with evidence appears in the courtroom; the child stamps it with the three tests and gives a verdict. Weak claims can be 'retried' after the child chooses better evidence from a list.","A team reads a claim card aloud as the 'lawyer'.","The 'judges' (another team) test it with the three stamps.","Judges give a verdict: STRONG or WEAK, with a reason.","Correct verdict = 2 points; naming the missing quality = 1 bonus point.","Teams rotate roles."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is an *allowance*?","answer":"money that parents give their children regularly","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مصروف الجيب.","options":["a type of bank loan","a school timetable","the price of a bicycle","money that parents give their children regularly"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *deadline*?","answer":"the date by which something must be done","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"موعد نهائي.","options":["a type of savings box","the date by which something must be done","a vague goal","a bank account"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Don't spend your money on **impulse**.* *On impulse* means ___.","answer":"suddenly, without thinking","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"باندفاع.","options":["after careful planning","with a parent","suddenly, without thinking","at the bank"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which question checks whether evidence is CREDIBLE?","answer":"Does it come from a trustworthy, named source?","explanation":"Credibility.","explanation_ar":"مصدر موثوق.","options":["Does it come from a trustworthy, named source?","Is it the longest sentence?","Is it written in bold?","Is it funny?"],"dok":1,"skill":"evaluating claims"},{"q":"Which sentence uses the first conditional correctly?","answer":"If you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after a year.","explanation":"First conditional.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["If you will save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after a year.","If you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after a year.","If you saved 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after a year.","If you save 10 riyals every week, you have had 520 riyals after a year."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Why is an *anecdote* usually NOT sufficient evidence for a general claim?","answer":"because it is only one person's experience","explanation":"Sufficiency.","explanation_ar":"تجربة شخص واحد.","options":["because it is always false","because it is only one person's experience","because it uses numbers","because it comes from the OECD"],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims"},{"q":"Which evidence is NOT relevant to the claim *Teenagers need to learn about money*?","answer":"Many teenagers enjoy playing football.","explanation":"Relevance.","explanation_ar":"غير ملائم.","options":["Most teenagers have bought something online.","Many teenagers have a payment card.","Many teenagers enjoy playing football.","Some teenagers do not recognize scams."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims"},{"q":"What is the main purpose of *How to Build Your First Savings Plan*?","answer":"to teach readers the steps for creating a savings plan","explanation":"Author's purpose.","explanation_ar":"تعليم خطوات خطة الادخار.","options":["to tell a story about a bicycle","to argue that banks are dangerous","to teach readers the steps for creating a savings plan","to describe the history of money"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** A writer claims that *all students waste their allowance*. Which choice BEST explains why this claim is weak?","answer":"It draws a conclusion about every student without sufficient evidence.","explanation":"Weak reasoning.","explanation_ar":"تعميم بلا دليل كافٍ.","options":["It uses a statistic from a credible organization.","It includes a verifiable calculation.","It explains its reasoning step by step.","It draws a conclusion about every student without sufficient evidence."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims"},{"q":"How does the procedure help answer *How can smart choices about money shape our future?*","answer":"It shows that small, regular choices, such as saving first and separating needs from wants, build toward bigger goals.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"الخيارات الصغيرة المنتظمة.","options":["It shows that small, regular choices, such as saving first and separating needs from wants, build toward bigger goals.","It shows that only adults can save.","It argues that money should never be shared.","It says that saving happens by luck."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'allowance': a parent handing coins to a child"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'track': a notebook with a list of daily expenses"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'deadline': a calendar with one date circled in red"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'vague': a blurry sign that is hard to read"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'impulse': a hand quickly grabbing candy at a checkout"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'anecdote': a person telling a short story to a friend"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hasty generalization': one small figure pointing to a huge crowd"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-01","prompt":"The six steps of a savings plan."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-02","prompt":"Saving 10 riyals a week for a year."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS3-03","prompt":"Testing evidence with three questions."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V01","script":"allowance. He saves half of his weekly allowance."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V02","script":"track. Track your spending for two weeks."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V03","script":"deadline. My deadline is the summer holiday."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V04","script":"vague. *Save money* is a vague goal."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V05","script":"impulse. He bought the game on impulse."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V06","script":"anecdote. One anecdote is not enough to prove a claim."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-V07","script":"hasty generalization. *My cousin lost money, so all saving is useless* is a hasty generalization."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-T1","script":"Saving money is not only for adults with large salaries. Anyone with a small income – a weekly allowance, Eid gifts or money from a summer job – can learn to save. A savings plan turns good intentions into a simple routine. Follow these six steps. **You will need:** a notebook or a simple budgeting app, a safe place to keep your savings and about fifteen minutes each week. **Step 1 – Set a clear goal.** Decide what you are saving for and how much it costs. *Save money* is vague; *Save 600 riyals for a bicycle by the summer holiday* is clear. A specific goal with a deadline is easier to stick to, because you can measure your progress. **Step 2 – Track your income and spending.** For two weeks, write down every riyal that comes in and every riyal that goes out. Many people are surprised to discover how much they spend on small things, such as snacks, mobile data and in-app purchases. **Step 3 – Separate needs from wants.** Look at your list and mark each expense N (need) or W (want). Needs, such as school supplies, come first. Wants are not forbidden, but they are the easiest place to cut back. **Step 4 – Pay yourself first.** As soon as you receive money, put your savings aside before you spend anything. Small amounts add up: if you save 10 riyals every week, you will have 520 riyals after one year. **Step 5 – Keep your savings safe.** Use a locked box at home or, with a parent's help, a youth bank account. Keeping savings out of easy reach makes it harder to spend them on impulse. **Step 6 – Review and reward.** Once a month, compare your progress with your goal. If you are behind, find one expense to reduce or extend your deadline a little. When you reach your goal, celebrate – and then set a new one. **Remember to share.** Many families include charity (*sadaqah*) in their plans. In Islam, a person whose savings stay above a minimum amount, called the *nisab*, for a full lunar year pays zakat of 2.5%. Giving is part of a balanced plan, not a break from it. **Warning:** Be careful of anyone who promises to double your savings quickly. If an offer sounds too good to be true, it probably is. Always ask a trusted adult before you put your money anywhere new."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS3-VID","script":"Ten riyals a week. Fifty-two weeks. Five hundred and twenty riyals. Small amounts add up! Set a goal. Track. Needs before wants. Pay yourself first. Keep it safe. Review. Is the evidence relevant? Is it sufficient? Is it credible? 'My cousin failed, so nobody can save!' – that's a hasty generalization. Double your money in a week? If it sounds too good to be true, it probably is."}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Counterclaims and rebuttals","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["state a counterclaim fairly and avoid a straw man","write rebuttals with three strategies: concede and outweigh, refute with evidence, offer a solution","use counterclaim signals and turn words accurately, and answer SAT-style questions on transitions"],"objectives_ar":["عرض الادعاء المضاد بإنصاف وتجنب مغالطة رجل القش.","كتابة ردود بثلاث استراتيجيات: الاعتراف ثم الترجيح، والدحض بالدليل، واقتراح حل.","استخدام إشارات الادعاء المضاد وكلمات الانعطاف بدقة، والإجابة عن أسئلة SAT في أدوات الربط."],"success_criteria":["I can explain why a straw man paragraph is weak.","I can write a counterclaim paragraph with a fair counterclaim, a turn word and a rebuttal that links back to my claim.","I can choose the best rebuttal strategy for a counterclaim."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع شرح سبب ضعف فقرة رجل القش.","أستطيع كتابة فقرة ادعاء مضاد منصفة بكلمة انعطاف ورد يرتبط بادعائي.","أستطيع اختيار أنسب استراتيجية للرد على ادعاء مضاد."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"opponent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpoʊnənt/","definition":"a person who disagrees with an idea or a plan","arabic":"معارض / خصم","example":"Opponents of the plan say it costs too much.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"contend","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈtɛnd/","definition":"to say that something is true, especially in an argument","arabic":"يزعم / يحتجّ بأن","example":"Critics contend that students are too young.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"admittedly","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/ədˈmɪtɪdli/","definition":"used to accept that something is true before adding another point","arabic":"من المسلَّم به / صحيح أن","example":"Admittedly, the plan takes time.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"nevertheless","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/ˌnɛvərðəˈlɛs/","definition":"in spite of what has just been said","arabic":"ومع ذلك","example":"It is expensive. Nevertheless, it is worth it.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"drawback","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdrɔˌbæk/","definition":"a disadvantage or a problem","arabic":"عيب / سلبية","example":"The main drawback is the cost.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"outweigh","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˌaʊtˈweɪ/","definition":"to be greater or more important than something else","arabic":"يفوق / يرجح على","example":"The benefits outweigh the costs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"straw man","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌstrɔ ˈmæn/","definition":"a weak, exaggerated version of an opponent's argument that is easy to attack","arabic":"مغالطة رجل القش","example":"Attacking a straw man does not convince readers.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"At Level 8, a counterclaim paragraph does more than mention the other side. First, **state the counterclaim fairly** – the way a sensible opponent would put it. If you exaggerate or mock the other side, you create a **straw man**, and thoughtful readers stop trusting you. Use **signals**: *Admittedly, … / Opponents contend that… / Critics may point out that… / Some parents worry that…*\n\nThen choose a **rebuttal strategy**. **(1) Concede and outweigh** – accept the drawback, then show that the benefits are greater (*Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback*). **(2) Refute with evidence** – show that the counterclaim is not true (*However, the evidence suggests otherwise…*). **(3) Offer a solution** – accept the concern and propose a way to fix it (*To solve this, the school could…*).\n\n**Turn words** show the change of direction: *However, Nevertheless, Even so, Yet, On the contrary* (only when you completely reject the idea). Finish by **linking back** to your claim (*This shows that… / In this way…*). In a five-paragraph argument essay, the counterclaim and rebuttal usually go in **paragraph 4**, just before the conclusion.","examples":["Fair counterclaim: ***Admittedly, a market day takes time away from regular lessons.***","Fair counterclaim: ***Opponents contend that young students cannot handle money responsibly.***","Straw man (avoid): ✗ *Some people say market days are a terrible idea because students only want to play.*","Concede and outweigh: ***Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback.***","Refute with evidence: ***However, our own experience suggests otherwise.***","Offer a solution: ***To solve it, the school could set a spending limit of 20 riyals per student.***","Link back: ***These results show that, with guidance, students can manage money carefully.***","*On the contrary* (complete rejection): ***Critics say saving is boring. On the contrary, many students enjoy watching their savings grow.***","*Even so* (accept, then continue): ***The course costs money. Even so, it saves families money in the long run.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Mocking the other side (*Only foolish people think…*). → ✓ State the view respectfully, as a sensible opponent would. (Avoid straw men.)","✗ Giving a counterclaim and no rebuttal. → ✓ Always answer it and link back to your claim. (Complete the move.)","✗ Using *On the contrary* after a concession. → ✓ Use *Nevertheless / Even so* after a concession. (Choose the right turn word.)","✗ Putting the counterclaim in the conclusion. → ✓ Put it in paragraph 4, before the conclusion. (Placement.)","✗ Admittedly, the plan is expensive, however it is worth it. → ✓ Admittedly, the plan is expensive; however, it is worth it. (Semicolon or full stop before *however*.)","✗ Rebutting with an opinion only (*I just think it's good*). → ✓ Rebut with evidence, a weighing of benefits or a solution. (Rebuttal strategy.)","✗ The benefits outweighs the costs. → ✓ The benefits outweigh the costs. (Plural subject: **outweigh**.)"],"arabic":"في المستوى الثامن لا تكتفي فقرة الادعاء المضاد بذكر الرأي الآخر؛ فأولًا اعرضه بإنصاف كما يعرضه معارض عاقل، لأن المبالغة فيه أو السخرية منه تصنع «رجل القش» فيفقد القارئ ثقته بك. واستخدم إشارات مثل Admittedly وOpponents contend that.\n\nثم اختر استراتيجية الرد: الاعتراف بالعيب ثم إظهار أن الفوائد أكبر، أو دحض الادعاء بالدليل، أو قبول القلق واقتراح حل له.\n\nكلمات الانعطاف مثل However وNevertheless وEven so وYet تُظهر تغيّر الاتجاه، وOn the contrary تُستخدم فقط عند الرفض التام. واختم بربط الرد بادعائك. وفي المقالة الجدلية الخماسية توضع فقرة الادعاء المضاد عادة رابعةً قبل الخاتمة.\n\nانتبه: ضع فاصلة منقوطة أو نقطة قبل however عند ربط جملتين مستقلتين."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:40","narration":"Knocking down a scarecrow is easy – but it won't convince anyone. Don't argue with a straw man!","on_screen":"Answering the Other Side ⚖️","visual":"A debater knocking over a scarecrow while the audience shrugs."},{"time":"00:40–01:30","narration":"State the other side fairly: Admittedly… Opponents contend that… Some parents worry that…","on_screen":"Fair counterclaims","visual":"A speaker respectfully presenting an opponent's view."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"Three ways to answer: concede and outweigh, refute with evidence, offer a solution.","on_screen":"Three rebuttal strategies","visual":"A balance scale, a magnifying glass and a toolbox appearing in turn."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Turn with However, Nevertheless, Even so – and always link back to your claim.","on_screen":"Turn words + link back","visual":"An arrow turning a corner and pointing back to the claim."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Paragraph 4 is your counterclaim paragraph. Make it fair, firm and focused.","on_screen":"Where does it go?","visual":"A five-paragraph essay outline with paragraph 4 glowing."}],"captions_ar":["إسقاط الفزّاعة سهل، لكنه لا يقنع أحدًا. لا تجادل رجل القش!","اعرض الرأي الآخر بإنصاف: Admittedly… Opponents contend that… Some parents worry that…","ثلاث طرق للرد: الاعتراف ثم الترجيح، والدحض بالدليل، واقتراح حل.","انعطف بـ However وNevertheless وEven so، واربط دائمًا بادعائك.","الفقرة الرابعة هي فقرة الادعاء المضاد: اجعلها منصفة وحازمة ومركّزة."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, lively, debate-coach male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Answering the Other Side: Reem's Counterclaim Paragraphs","body":"**Reem's claim:** *Every school should hold a student market day once a term, where students plan, make and sell their own products.*\n\n**Paragraph A (weak – a straw man):** *Some people say market days are a terrible idea because students only want to play and never learn anything. This is obviously wrong. Market days are fun.*\n\n**Paragraph B (strong – concede and outweigh):** Admittedly, a market day takes time away from regular lessons, and teachers already have a full timetable. Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback. In a single day, students practice mathematics by calculating costs and profits, writing by designing advertisements and teamwork by running a stall together. One day each term is a small price for so many skills.\n\n**Paragraph C (strong – refute with evidence):** Opponents contend that young students cannot handle money responsibly. However, our own experience suggests otherwise. When our class ran a trial market last year, every team kept a written record of its income and expenses, and together we made a profit of 1,240 riyals, which we donated to a local charity. These results show that, with guidance, students can manage money carefully.\n\n**Paragraph D (strong – offer a solution):** Some parents worry that children will spend too much money buying from other stalls. This is a fair concern. To solve it, the school could set a spending limit of 20 riyals per student and use paper tokens instead of cash. In this way, students would still learn to make choices without spending too much.","word_count":249,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"900L–1050L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Paragraph A is a straw man:** it exaggerates the other side (*only want to play, never learn anything*), insults it (*obviously wrong*) and gives no evidence. Readers who disagree will not be convinced.","**State the counterclaim fairly**, as a sensible opponent would: *Admittedly, a market day takes time away from regular lessons.*","**Signal the other side:** *Admittedly…, Opponents contend that…, Some parents worry that…, Critics may point out that…*","**Strategy 1 – concede and outweigh (Paragraph B):** accept the drawback, then show that the benefits are greater – *Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback.*","**Strategy 2 – refute with evidence (Paragraph C):** show that the counterclaim is not true – *However, our own experience suggests otherwise.* (One class trial is local evidence; data from many schools would make it even stronger.)","**Strategy 3 – offer a solution (Paragraph D):** accept the concern and solve it – *To solve it, the school could…*","**Link back to the claim** at the end of each rebuttal: *These results show that…, In this way, students would still learn…*"]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Reem's paragraphs. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ فقرات ريم واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What is Reem's claim?","answer":"Every school should hold a student market day once a term.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["Every school should hold a student market day once a term.","Market days are a terrible idea.","Students should not handle money.","Teachers have a full timetable."],"dok":1,"skill":"claim","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why is Paragraph A weak?","answer":"It exaggerates and mocks the other side and gives no evidence.","explanation":"Straw man.","explanation_ar":"رجل القش.","options":["It is too long.","It uses too many statistics.","It exaggerates and mocks the other side and gives no evidence.","It offers a solution."],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which drawback does Reem concede in Paragraph B?","answer":"A market day takes time away from regular lessons.","explanation":"Concession.","explanation_ar":"يأخذ وقتًا من الدروس.","options":["A market day takes time away from regular lessons.","Students only want to play.","Parents will spend too much.","The profit was too small."],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How much profit did Reem's class make at the trial market?","answer":"1,240 riyals","explanation":"Paragraph C.","explanation_ar":"1240 ريالًا.","options":["20 riyals","124 riyals","12,400 riyals","1,240 riyals"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What solution does Reem offer to parents' worries in Paragraph D?","answer":"a spending limit of 20 riyals and paper tokens instead of cash","explanation":"Paragraph D.","explanation_ar":"حد إنفاق ورموز ورقية.","options":["cancelling the market day","giving every student 100 riyals","holding the market at night","a spending limit of 20 riyals and paper tokens instead of cash"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which phrase signals the counterclaim in Paragraph C?","answer":"Opponents contend that","explanation":"Signals.","explanation_ar":"Opponents contend that.","options":["These results show that","Opponents contend that","In a single day","To solve it"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Rebuttal strategies and SAT-style questions. Choose.","instructions_ar":"استراتيجيات الرد وأسئلة SAT: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which rebuttal strategy does Paragraph B use?","answer":"concede and outweigh","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"الاعتراف ثم الترجيح.","options":["refute with evidence","offer a solution","concede and outweigh","attack a straw man"],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttals","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which rebuttal strategy is BEST for the counterclaim *Some students may feel embarrassed if their stall makes a loss*?","answer":"Offer a solution, such as teaching students that losses are part of learning and letting teams share results privately.","explanation":"Choosing a strategy.","explanation_ar":"اقتراح حل.","options":["Say that only weak students make losses.","Ignore the counterclaim.","Say that market days are fun.","Offer a solution, such as teaching students that losses are part of learning and letting teams share results privately."],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttals","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence states a counterclaim FAIRLY?","answer":"Critics may point out that organizing a market day requires extra work from teachers.","explanation":"Fair counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"عرض منصف.","options":["Lazy people hate market days.","Critics may point out that organizing a market day requires extra work from teachers.","Anyone who disagrees with me is wrong.","Only boring teachers would complain."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which counterclaim sentence uses *however* with correct punctuation?","answer":"Admittedly, the plan costs money; however, the benefits are greater.","explanation":"Punctuation with however.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Admittedly, the plan costs money; however, the benefits are greater.","Admittedly the plan costs money, however the benefits are greater.","Admittedly, the plan costs money however, the benefits are greater.","Admittedly, the plan costs money, however; the benefits are greater."],"dok":2,"skill":"conventions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Some students argue that saving is pointless because prices keep rising. ______, regular saving still gives them choices that they would not have otherwise.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"Even so","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"Even so.","options":["For instance","Similarly","As a result","Even so"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Critics claim that students will lose interest in a savings club after a few weeks. ______, membership in our club has grown every month since it began.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"On the contrary","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"On the contrary.","options":["On the contrary","Admittedly","Likewise","In addition"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the counterclaim paragraph with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل فقرة الادعاء المضاد من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________, a savings club needs a teacher to supervise it.","answer":"Admittedly","explanation":"Concession.","explanation_ar":"من المسلَّم به.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, the benefits for students are worth the effort.","answer":"Nevertheless","explanation":"Turn word.","explanation_ar":"ومع ذلك.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The main ________ of the plan is the extra time it takes.","answer":"drawback","explanation":"Disadvantage.","explanation_ar":"عيب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"I believe the advantages ________ the costs.","answer":"outweigh","explanation":"Are greater than.","explanation_ar":"تفوق.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ of the club say it is too expensive to run.","answer":"Opponents","explanation":"People who disagree.","explanation_ar":"المعارضون.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Some parents ________ that children are too young to manage money.","answer":"contend","explanation":"Say firmly.","explanation_ar":"يزعمون.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write counterclaims and rebuttals.","instructions_ar":"اكتب ادعاءات مضادة وردودًا.","items":[{"q":"Rewrite Paragraph A so that it states the counterclaim fairly and answers it.","answer":"Some people worry that students will treat market day as a holiday rather than a lesson. However, when each team must plan a budget and record its income and expenses, the day becomes a practical lesson in mathematics and responsibility.","explanation":"Rewrite.","explanation_ar":"أعد الكتابة.","dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a concede-and-outweigh rebuttal to: *Financial education will make the school day longer.*","answer":"Admittedly, adding a new subject takes time. Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback, because students gain skills they will use every day of their adult lives.","explanation":"Concede and outweigh.","explanation_ar":"اعترف ورجّح.","dok":3,"skill":"rebuttals","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a solution rebuttal to: *Students might lose their savings if they keep them at school.*","answer":"This is a fair concern. To solve it, the savings club could keep records only, while students keep their money safely at home or in a youth account with a parent.","explanation":"Offer a solution.","explanation_ar":"اقترح حلًا.","dok":3,"skill":"rebuttals","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain why fair counterclaims make an argument MORE convincing, not less.","answer":"They show readers that the writer understands the other side and has thought carefully, so readers who disagree are more likely to listen to the rebuttal.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":3,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Rebuttal Relay","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Rebuttal runner: a counterclaim appears as an obstacle on a track; the child picks a strategy, a turn word and a link-back phrase to build a bridge. Fair, complete rebuttals let the runner cross; straw-man answers make the bridge collapse.","Teams line up behind a whiteboard.","Runner 1 reads the counterclaim and chooses a strategy card.","Runner 2 writes the turn word and rebuttal sentence.","Runner 3 writes the link back to the claim.","Complete, fair rebuttal = 3 points; straw man = 0 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *straw man* in an argument?","answer":"a weak, exaggerated version of an opponent's view that is easy to attack","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"رجل القش.","options":["a strong piece of evidence","a weak, exaggerated version of an opponent's view that is easy to attack","the writer's main claim","a type of conclusion"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"What is a *drawback*?","answer":"a disadvantage or problem","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"عيب.","options":["a type of profit","a turn word","an opponent","a disadvantage or problem"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The benefits **outweigh** the costs.* *Outweigh* means ___.","answer":"are greater or more important than","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تفوق.","options":["are smaller than","are equal to","are greater or more important than","hide"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word is used to accept a point before adding another one?","answer":"Admittedly","explanation":"Signals.","explanation_ar":"Admittedly.","options":["Therefore","Firstly","Finally","Admittedly"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterclaims"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *outweigh* correctly?","answer":"The benefits of saving outweigh the small sacrifices.","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"outweigh.","options":["The benefits of saving outweighs the small sacrifices.","The benefits of saving outweigh the small sacrifices.","The benefits of saving outweighing the small sacrifices.","The benefits of saving is outweigh the small sacrifices."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which rebuttal strategy shows that the counterclaim is NOT true?","answer":"refute with evidence","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"الدحض بالدليل.","options":["refute with evidence","concede and outweigh","offer a solution","ignore it"],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttals"},{"q":"Where does the counterclaim paragraph usually go in a five-paragraph argument essay?","answer":"paragraph 4, before the conclusion","explanation":"Placement.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الرابعة.","options":["the first sentence of the introduction","after the conclusion","paragraph 4, before the conclusion","in the title"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure"},{"q":"Which turn word is BEST after a concession?","answer":"Nevertheless","explanation":"Turn words.","explanation_ar":"Nevertheless.","options":["On the contrary","For example","Nevertheless","Similarly"],"dok":2,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Opponents contend that market days are too noisy for the school. ______, the market could be held in the sports hall, where noise would not disturb classes.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"To address this concern","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"لمعالجة هذا القلق.","options":["For this reason","To address this concern","In other words","Similarly"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"Why does answering counterclaims fairly matter when making choices about money?","answer":"It helps us weigh real drawbacks and benefits before making decisions that affect our future.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"موازنة السلبيات والإيجابيات.","options":["It helps us weigh real drawbacks and benefits before making decisions that affect our future.","It means we never change our minds.","It lets us ignore other people's concerns.","It makes every choice risk-free."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'opponent': two debaters facing each other"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'contend': a speaker making a firm point with one finger raised"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'admittedly': a person nodding with an open hand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'nevertheless': a hiker continuing up a hill in the rain"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drawback': a scale with a small weight labeled MINUS"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'outweigh': a balance scale tipping toward BENEFITS"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'straw man': a scarecrow being knocked over by a debater"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-01","prompt":"A straw man compared with a fair argument."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-02","prompt":"The three rebuttal strategies."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS4-03","prompt":"A student market day with a spending limit."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V01","script":"opponent. Opponents of the plan say it costs too much."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V02","script":"contend. Critics contend that students are too young."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V03","script":"admittedly. Admittedly, the plan takes time."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V04","script":"nevertheless. It is expensive. Nevertheless, it is worth it."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V05","script":"drawback. The main drawback is the cost."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V06","script":"outweigh. The benefits outweigh the costs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-V07","script":"straw man. Attacking a straw man does not convince readers."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-T1","script":"**Reem's claim:** *Every school should hold a student market day once a term, where students plan, make and sell their own products.* **Paragraph A (weak – a straw man):** *Some people say market days are a terrible idea because students only want to play and never learn anything. This is obviously wrong. Market days are fun.* **Paragraph B (strong – concede and outweigh):** Admittedly, a market day takes time away from regular lessons, and teachers already have a full timetable. Nevertheless, the benefits outweigh this drawback. In a single day, students practice mathematics by calculating costs and profits, writing by designing advertisements and teamwork by running a stall together. One day each term is a small price for so many skills. **Paragraph C (strong – refute with evidence):** Opponents contend that young students cannot handle money responsibly. However, our own experience suggests otherwise. When our class ran a trial market last year, every team kept a written record of its income and expenses, and together we made a profit of 1,240 riyals, which we donated to a local charity. These results show that, with guidance, students can manage money carefully. **Paragraph D (strong – offer a solution):** Some parents worry that children will spend too much money buying from other stalls. This is a fair concern. To solve it, the school could set a spending limit of 20 riyals per student and use paper tokens instead of cash. In this way, students would still learn to make choices without spending too much."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Knocking down a scarecrow is easy – but it won't convince anyone. Don't argue with a straw man! State the other side fairly: Admittedly… Opponents contend that… Some parents worry that… Three ways to answer: concede and outweigh, refute with evidence, offer a solution. Turn with However, Nevertheless, Even so – and always link back to your claim. Paragraph 4 is your counterclaim paragraph. Make it fair, firm and focused."}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A five-paragraph argument essay","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a five-paragraph argument essay on a money topic with the Argument Essay Planner","write 250–300 words with a clear claim, two supported reasons, a fair counterclaim with a rebuttal and a strong conclusion","revise for evidence and fairness, edit and present the essay in a Money Matters Debate"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لمقالة جدلية من خمس فقرات عن موضوع مالي باستخدام مخطط المقالة الجدلية.","كتابة 250–300 كلمة بادعاء واضح وسببين مدعومين وادعاء مضاد منصف مع رد وخاتمة قوية.","تحسين الأدلة والإنصاف وتدقيق المقالة وعرضها في مناظرة قضايا المال."],"success_criteria":["My claim is clear and my two reasons are supported by relevant evidence.","My counterclaim is fair, and my rebuttal uses one strategy and links back to my claim.","My essay is 250–300 words in five paragraphs, with correct collocations and punctuation."],"success_criteria_ar":["ادعائي واضح وسبباي مدعومان بأدلة ملائمة.","ادعائي المضاد منصف وردّي يستخدم استراتيجية ويرتبط بادعائي.","مقالتي 250–300 كلمة في خمس فقرات بمتلازمات وترقيم صحيحين."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"position","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈzɪʃən/","definition":"an opinion or a side in an argument","arabic":"موقف / رأي","example":"Decide your position before you plan.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"preview","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpriˌvju/","definition":"a short look at what will come next","arabic":"لمحة مسبقة / تمهيد","example":"The thesis gives a preview of the reasons.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"developer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈvɛləpər/","definition":"a person or a company that creates software or games","arabic":"مطوّر","example":"Game developers earn money from purchases.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"reliability","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˌlaɪəˈbɪləti/","definition":"the quality of being trusted to do what is expected","arabic":"الاعتمادية / الموثوقية","example":"A first job teaches reliability.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"canteen","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kænˈtin/","definition":"a place in a school where students buy food and drinks","arabic":"مقصف","example":"The canteen accepts digital payments.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"call to reflection","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɔl tu rɪˈflɛkʃən/","definition":"a final sentence that asks readers to think about the issue","arabic":"دعوة إلى التأمل","example":"End with a call to reflection or a call to action.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"persuasive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/pərˈsweɪsɪv/","definition":"able to make people agree or do something","arabic":"مقنع","example":"Fair counterclaims make an essay more persuasive.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Choose a card and take a clear **position**. Your **introduction** has three parts: a **hook** (a question, a surprising fact or a short scene), **background** (why the issue matters now) and your **claim** with a **preview** of your two reasons. Each **body paragraph** (2 and 3) starts with a topic sentence that states one reason, then gives **evidence** and **explains** it (*This shows that…*).\n\n**Paragraph 4** is your **counterclaim paragraph**. State the other side fairly (*Admittedly…, Opponents contend that…*), then choose a strategy: **outweigh** the drawback, **refute** it with evidence or **offer a solution**. Finish by linking back to your claim. **Paragraph 5** restates your claim in new words, sums up your reasons and ends with a **call to action** (*Schools should…*) or a **call to reflection** (*Before you tap 'buy', ask yourself…*).\n\nMake your essay **persuasive** with **relevant** evidence from the card (name the source), precise **finance collocations** (*make a profit, save up for, cut back on*) and **transitions** (*First, Moreover, Admittedly, Nevertheless, In conclusion*). Check that every claim you make is supported – a single anecdote is not enough.","examples":["Hook (question): ***What is the best way to learn how to swim – reading about water, or getting into the pool?***","Claim + preview: ***Every teenager should receive a regular allowance, because it builds budgeting skills and teaches responsibility safely.***","Topic sentence (reason 1): ***First, an allowance turns money lessons into real practice.***","Evidence with a source: ***The OECD found that students who have worked on financial tasks tend to perform better in financial literacy.***","Explanation: ***This shows that knowledge grows fastest when students use it.***","Counterclaim: ***Admittedly, not every family can afford to give a large allowance.***","Rebuttal (offer a solution): ***However, the amount can be small, and the habits it builds are the same.***","Call to action: ***Families should start small, start early and let teenagers learn by doing.***","Call to reflection: ***Before you tap 'buy', ask yourself: who really profits from this purchase?***"],"common_errors":["✗ A claim that is a fact (*Many teenagers get an allowance*). → ✓ Make it arguable (*Every teenager should receive an allowance*). (Arguable claim.)","✗ Three reasons in one body paragraph. → ✓ One reason per body paragraph. (Organization.)","✗ Evidence with no explanation. → ✓ Add *This shows that… / In other words…* (Explain evidence.)","✗ A straw man in paragraph 4. → ✓ State the counterclaim as a sensible opponent would. (Fairness.)","✗ New reasons in the conclusion. → ✓ Restate and sum up; add no new evidence. (Conclusion.)","✗ Teenagers should earn money to spend it in useful things. → ✓ Teenagers should earn money and spend it on useful things. (spend **on**.)","✗ In conclusion, as I said before, allowance is good. → ✓ In conclusion, a regular allowance prepares teenagers for a lifetime of smart choices. (Restate in new, precise words.)"],"arabic":"اختر بطاقة واتخذ موقفًا واضحًا. وللمقدمة ثلاثة أجزاء: افتتاحية (سؤال أو حقيقة مدهشة أو مشهد قصير)، وخلفية تبيّن أهمية القضية الآن، وادعاؤك مع لمحة عن سببيك. وتبدأ كل فقرة عرض بجملة افتتاحية تذكر سببًا واحدًا، ثم الدليل وشرحه.\n\nالفقرة الرابعة للادعاء المضاد: اعرض الرأي الآخر بإنصاف، ثم اختر استراتيجية: الترجيح أو الدحض بالدليل أو اقتراح حل، واربط بادعائك. والفقرة الخامسة تعيد الادعاء بكلمات جديدة وتلخّص الأسباب وتنتهي بدعوة إلى العمل أو إلى التأمل.\n\nاجعل مقالتك مقنعة بأدلة ملائمة من البطاقة مع ذكر المصدر، ومتلازمات مالية دقيقة، وأدوات ربط مناسبة، وتأكد أن كل ادعاء مدعوم، فالحكاية الفردية وحدها لا تكفي.\n\nانتبه: spend money on، ولا تضف أدلة جديدة في الخاتمة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:40","narration":"Allowances, summer jobs, digital canteens, young entrepreneurs, in-app purchases – pick your debate!","on_screen":"Money Matters Debate 🎤","visual":"Five debate cards fanning out on a table."},{"time":"00:40–01:40","narration":"Hook, background, claim and preview – then one reason per body paragraph, with evidence and explanation.","on_screen":"Paragraphs 1–3","visual":"An essay outline filling in paragraph by paragraph."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Paragraph 4: admit, turn, rebut, link back.","on_screen":"The counterclaim paragraph","visual":"A balance scale, a magnifying glass and a toolbox."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Restate, sum up and end with a call to action – or a call to reflection.","on_screen":"Paragraph 5","visual":"A speaker finishing a speech as the audience nods."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Revise for evidence and fairness, then take the stage at the Money Matters Debate!","on_screen":"Revise and present","visual":"Students at two podiums in a classroom debate."}],"captions_ar":["المصروف، والعمل الصيفي، والمقصف الرقمي، ورواد الأعمال الصغار، والمشتريات داخل الألعاب: اختر مناظرتك!","افتتاحية وخلفية وادعاء ولمحة، ثم سبب واحد في كل فقرة عرض مع دليل وشرح.","الفقرة الرابعة: اعترف، ثم انعطف، ثم رُدّ، ثم اربط.","أعد الادعاء ولخّص واختم بدعوة إلى العمل أو إلى التأمل.","حسّن الأدلة والإنصاف، ثم اصعد المنصة في مناظرة قضايا المال!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, confident, motivating female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Money Debate Topic Cards","body":"**Card A – Should every teenager receive a regular allowance?** · For: it gives practice in budgeting, saving and making choices; mistakes with small amounts teach lessons safely · Against: some families cannot afford it; children may waste it or expect money without effort · Fact: the OECD found that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy\n\n**Card B – Should teenagers aged 15 and over be allowed to take part-time summer jobs?** · For: they earn an income, learn responsibility and build skills such as communication and time management · Against: work may tire students or take time from family, rest and summer study · Fact: many employers say that young people's first jobs teach reliability and teamwork\n\n**Card C – Should school canteens accept only digital payments?** · For: faster queues, no lost coins, and parents can track spending · Against: some students may not have a card, and digital money can feel less 'real', so students may spend more · Fact: according to the Saudi Central Bank (SAMA), electronic payments made up 79% of retail payments in Saudi Arabia in 2024, up from 70% in 2023\n\n**Card D – Should entrepreneurship be taught in middle school?** · For: students learn to plan, take reasonable risks, solve problems and understand profit and loss · Against: the timetable is crowded; not every student wants to start a business · Fact: entrepreneurship programs often combine mathematics, writing and teamwork in real projects\n\n**Card E – Should games for children under 16 be allowed to sell in-app purchases?** · For: developers need income, and players can choose not to buy · Against: purchases can be designed to encourage impulse spending, and young players may not understand the real cost · Fact: in the OECD's PISA 2022 study, more than 85% of 15-year-olds in the participating OECD countries had bought something online in the previous year","word_count":303,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L08-U3-LS5-1","genre":"topic cards","role":"model","difficulty":"900L–1050L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one card** and decide your **position**. You will write a **five-paragraph argument essay** of **250–300 words**.","Plan: (1) **introduction** – hook, background, claim with a preview of reasons; (2) **reason 1** with evidence; (3) **reason 2** with evidence; (4) **counterclaim and rebuttal** – state the other side fairly and use one strategy (outweigh, refute or solve); (5) **conclusion** – restate the claim in new words and end with a call to action or a memorable line.","Use **at least one piece of evidence from the card** (with its source if it has one), **two finance collocations** and **one counterclaim signal** (*Admittedly…, Opponents contend that…*)."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the topic cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ بطاقات المواضيع والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"According to Card C, what percentage of retail payments in Saudi Arabia were electronic in 2024?","answer":"79%","explanation":"Card C.","explanation_ar":"79%.","options":["70%","79%","85%","18%"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which organization reported the figure in Card C?","answer":"the Saudi Central Bank (SAMA)","explanation":"Card C.","explanation_ar":"البنك المركزي السعودي.","options":["the OECD","the World Health Organization","a game developer","the Saudi Central Bank (SAMA)"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which point AGAINST in-app purchases appears in Card E?","answer":"Purchases can be designed to encourage impulse spending.","explanation":"Card E.","explanation_ar":"تشجيع الشراء الاندفاعي.","options":["Developers need income.","Players can choose not to buy.","Purchases can be designed to encourage impulse spending.","Games are always free."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which skill do first jobs teach, according to Card B?","answer":"reliability and teamwork","explanation":"Card B.","explanation_ar":"الاعتمادية والعمل الجماعي.","options":["how to avoid all work","reliability and teamwork","only mathematics","how to win games"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What image does the model essay use as its hook?","answer":"learning to swim by getting into the pool","explanation":"Model, P1.","explanation_ar":"تعلّم السباحة.","options":["a bicycle in a shop window","a fake prize message","a crowded canteen","learning to swim by getting into the pool"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which rebuttal strategy does the model essay use in paragraph 4?","answer":"offer a solution","explanation":"Model, P4.","explanation_ar":"اقتراح حل.","options":["offer a solution","refute with evidence","attack a straw man","ignore the counterclaim"],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttals","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your essay. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطط لمقالتك: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST claim for an essay on Card C?","answer":"School canteens should accept only digital payments, because they make queues faster and help parents track spending.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"أفضل ادعاء.","options":["School canteens should accept only digital payments, because they make queues faster and help parents track spending.","Many people use digital payments.","This essay is about canteens.","SAMA is the Saudi Central Bank."],"dok":2,"skill":"claim","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a FAIR counterclaim for an essay supporting Card D?","answer":"Opponents contend that the timetable is already crowded and not every student wants to start a business.","explanation":"Fair counterclaim.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء منصف.","options":["Only lazy students would dislike entrepreneurship.","Anyone who disagrees doesn't understand money.","Entrepreneurship is obviously perfect.","Opponents contend that the timetable is already crowded and not every student wants to start a business."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterclaims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses evidence from Card E with its source?","answer":"In the OECD's PISA 2022 study, more than 85% of 15-year-olds in the participating OECD countries had bought something online in the previous year.","explanation":"Evidence + source.","explanation_ar":"دليل مع مصدره.","options":["Lots of kids buy stuff.","I think most teenagers shop online.","In the OECD's PISA 2022 study, more than 85% of 15-year-olds in the participating OECD countries had bought something online in the previous year.","My cousin buys games every week."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence BEST explains a piece of evidence?","answer":"This shows that teenagers are already making buying decisions and need guidance.","explanation":"Explanation.","explanation_ar":"شرح الدليل.","options":["This is a statistic.","This shows that teenagers are already making buying decisions and need guidance.","The OECD is an organization.","Here is another fact."],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Digital payments make queues faster. ______, they allow parents to see exactly what their children buy.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"Moreover","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"Moreover.","options":["However","For instance","Moreover","Instead"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes: *• E-payments: 79% of Saudi retail payments in 2024 (SAMA). • 70% in 2023. • Many shops accept cards.* The student wants to emphasize how quickly digital payments are growing. Which choice MOST effectively uses relevant information from the notes?","answer":"According to SAMA, electronic payments rose from 70% of Saudi retail payments in 2023 to 79% in 2024.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"يبرز سرعة النمو.","options":["According to SAMA, electronic payments rose from 70% of Saudi retail payments in 2023 to 79% in 2024.","Many shops accept cards.","SAMA publishes information about payments.","Electronic payments exist in Saudi Arabia."],"dok":3,"skill":"rhetorical synthesis","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan with the Argument Essay Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط بمخطط المقالة الجدلية.","items":[{"q":"Card C: write a hook, background and a claim with a preview.","answer":"Hook: Have you ever waited in a long canteen queue while someone searched for coins? Background: most payments in Saudi Arabia are now electronic. Claim: School canteens should accept only digital payments, because they save time and help parents track spending.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"المقدمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card C: write notes for reason 1 and reason 2 with evidence.","answer":"Reason 1: faster queues and no lost coins – more time to eat and rest. Reason 2: parents can track spending – SAMA: 79% of retail payments were electronic in 2024, so students will need these skills.","explanation":"Body notes.","explanation_ar":"ملاحظات العرض.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card C: write a fair counterclaim and choose a rebuttal strategy.","answer":"Counterclaim: Admittedly, some students may not have a card. Strategy – offer a solution: the school could provide free prepaid canteen cards that parents top up.","explanation":"Counterclaim plan.","explanation_ar":"خطة الادعاء المضاد.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Card C: write a conclusion with a call to action.","answer":"Digital canteens prepare students for the way money really works today. Schools should introduce prepaid cards this year so that every student can benefit.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and present your essay.","instructions_ar":"اكتب مقالتك وحسّنها ودققها واعرضها.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1 (hook, background, claim with preview).","answer":"Have you ever waited in a long canteen queue while someone searched for coins? Today, most payments in Saudi Arabia are electronic. School canteens should accept only digital payments, because they save time and help parents track spending.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة 1.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraphs 2 and 3 (two reasons with evidence and explanation).","answer":"First, digital payments make queues faster, so students have more time to eat and rest. Moreover, parents can track what their children buy. According to SAMA, 79% of Saudi retail payments were electronic in 2024. This shows that students need to learn to manage digital money now.","explanation":"Paragraphs 2–3.","explanation_ar":"الفقرتان 2–3.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 4 (fair counterclaim and rebuttal) and Paragraph 5 (conclusion).","answer":"Admittedly, some students may not have a bank card. To solve this, the school could provide free prepaid canteen cards that parents top up. In conclusion, digital canteens prepare students for the real world, and schools should introduce them this year.","explanation":"Paragraphs 4–5.","explanation_ar":"الفقرتان 4–5.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one place where you made your counterclaim fairer during revision (before → after).","answer":"Before: Some people foolishly think cash is better. After: Admittedly, some families prefer cash because it helps children see exactly how much they are spending.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"التحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Argument Essay Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose one card and a position. Plan each paragraph and the evidence you will use.","items":[{"q":"Hook + background + claim with preview","answer":"Hook: learning to swim in a pool, not from a book. Claim: Every teenager should receive a regular allowance, because it builds budgeting skills and teaches responsibility safely."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence + explanation","answer":"Practice: planning, saving up, comparing prices; OECD – students who work on financial tasks perform better."},{"q":"Reason 2 + evidence + explanation","answer":"Responsibility: small mistakes teach lessons safely; running out of money early teaches planning."},{"q":"Counterclaim (fair) + rebuttal strategy","answer":"Admittedly, not every family can afford a large allowance → offer a solution: a small amount or one linked to household responsibilities."},{"q":"Conclusion – restated claim + call to action or reflection","answer":"Start small, start early and let teenagers learn by doing."},{"q":"Evidence from the card and two finance collocations","answer":"OECD finding (Card A); save up for, run out of, spend money on"}]},{"id":"RW-L08-U3-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one Money Debate Topic Card and decide your position. Write a five-paragraph argument essay (250–300 words): (1) an introduction with a hook, background and a claim with a preview of two reasons; (2) reason 1 with evidence and explanation; (3) reason 2 with evidence and explanation; (4) a fair counterclaim and a rebuttal using one strategy; (5) a conclusion with a call to action or reflection. Use at least one piece of evidence from the card and two finance collocations. Choose one Money Debate Topic Card and decide your position. Write a five-paragraph argument essay (250–300 words): (1) an introduction with a hook, background and a claim with a preview of two reasons; (2) reason 1 with evidence and explanation; (3) reason 2 with evidence and explanation; (4) a fair counterclaim and a rebuttal using one strategy; (5) a conclusion with a call to action or reflection. Use at least one piece of evidence from the card and two finance collocations.","instructions_ar":"اختر بطاقة من بطاقات مناظرات المال وحدّد موقفك، واكتب مقالة جدلية من خمس فقرات (250–300 كلمة): (1) مقدمة بافتتاحية وخلفية وادعاء مع لمحة عن سببين، (2) السبب الأول بدليل وشرح، (3) السبب الثاني بدليل وشرح، (4) ادعاء مضاد منصف ورد باستراتيجية واحدة، (5) خاتمة بدعوة إلى العمل أو التأمل. استخدم دليلًا واحدًا على الأقل من البطاقة ومتلازمتين ماليتين.","prompt":"Choose one Money Debate Topic Card and decide your position. Write a five-paragraph argument essay (250–300 words): (1) an introduction with a hook, background and a claim with a preview of two reasons; (2) reason 1 with evidence and explanation; (3) reason 2 with evidence and explanation; (4) a fair counterclaim and a rebuttal using one strategy; (5) a conclusion with a call to action or reflection. Use at least one piece of evidence from the card and two finance collocations.","checklist":["My introduction has a hook, background and an arguable claim with a preview of two reasons.","Each body paragraph has a topic sentence with one reason.","I supported my reasons with relevant evidence and named the source when there was one.","I explained my evidence (This shows that… / In other words…).","My counterclaim is fair, with a signal (Admittedly…, Opponents contend that…).","My rebuttal uses one strategy (outweigh, refute or solve) and links back to my claim.","My conclusion restates the claim in new words and ends with a call to action or reflection.","My essay is 250–300 words; I used two finance collocations and checked punctuation and spelling."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":250,"max_words":330,"organizer":"Argument Essay Planner (hook – claim – reason 1 – reason 2 – counterclaim and rebuttal – conclusion – evidence)","items":[{"q":"Choose one Money Debate Topic Card and decide your position. Write a five-paragraph argument essay (250–300 words): (1) an introduction with a hook, background and a claim with a preview of two reasons; (2) reason 1 with evidence and explanation; (3) reason 2 with evidence and explanation; (4) a fair counterclaim and a rebuttal using one strategy; (5) a conclusion with a call to action or reflection. Use at least one piece of evidence from the card and two finance collocations.","answer":"What is the best way to learn how to swim – reading about water, or getting into the pool? Money works in the same way. Many teenagers study percentages at school, but few practice managing real money. **Every teenager should receive a regular allowance, because it builds budgeting skills and teaches responsibility safely.** (new paragraph) First, an allowance turns money lessons into real practice. With a fixed amount each week, teenagers must plan, save up for bigger goals and compare prices before they buy. According to the OECD, students who have worked on financial tasks tend to perform better in financial literacy. This shows that knowledge grows fastest when students actually use it. (new paragraph) Moreover, an allowance teaches responsibility while the stakes are still small. A student who spends everything on snacks and runs out of money by Wednesday learns an important lesson, and it costs only a few riyals. It is far better to learn this at thirteen than at thirty, when the mistakes are much more expensive. (new paragraph) Admittedly, not every family can afford to give a large allowance, and some parents worry that children will expect money without effort. However, the amount can be small, and families can link it to responsibilities at home, such as helping with younger siblings. In this way, the allowance teaches both planning and the value of effort. (new paragraph) In conclusion, a regular allowance is one of the simplest and most practical ways to prepare teenagers for a lifetime of smart choices. Families should start small, start early and let teenagers learn by doing.","_w":{"checklist":["My introduction has a hook, background and an arguable claim with a preview of two reasons.","Each body paragraph has a topic sentence with one reason.","I supported my reasons with relevant evidence and named the source when there was one.","I explained my evidence (This shows that… / In other words…).","My counterclaim is fair, with a signal (Admittedly…, Opponents contend that…).","My rebuttal uses one strategy (outweigh, refute or solve) and links back to my claim.","My conclusion restates the claim in new words and ends with a call to action or reflection.","My essay is 250–300 words; I used two finance collocations and checked punctuation and spelling."],"min":250,"max":330}}]}],"game":{"name":"Money Matters Debate","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Debate stage: the child chooses a card and a position, then builds a claim, picks the strongest evidence and answers a counterclaim from an AI 'opponent'. Fair, evidence-based answers fill the audience applause meter.","Two students with opposite positions on the same card come to the podiums.","Each reads their claim and one reason with evidence (1 minute).","Each reads their counterclaim paragraph (1 minute).","The class scores each speaker on claim, evidence and fairness (1–3 points each).","The fairest, best-supported speaker wins the round."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is your *position* in an argument?","answer":"the side or opinion you take","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"الموقف.","options":["the number of paragraphs","the side or opinion you take","the title of the essay","the source of a statistic"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What does a *preview* in the thesis do?","answer":"It briefly tells the reader the reasons that will follow.","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"يمهّد للأسباب.","options":["It ends the essay.","It gives the counterclaim.","It briefly tells the reader the reasons that will follow.","It lists all the evidence."],"dok":1,"skill":"essay structure"},{"q":"*Fair counterclaims make an essay more **persuasive**.* *Persuasive* means ___.","answer":"able to make people agree","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مقنع.","options":["very long","difficult to read","full of numbers","able to make people agree"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is a *canteen*?","answer":"a place in a school where students buy food and drinks","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مقصف.","options":["a type of bank card","a game developer","a savings box","a place in a school where students buy food and drinks"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *spend* with the correct preposition in a conclusion?","answer":"Teenagers who plan carefully spend their money on what matters most.","explanation":"spend on.","explanation_ar":"spend on.","options":["Teenagers who plan carefully spend their money on what matters most.","Teenagers who plan carefully spend their money in what matters most.","Teenagers who plan carefully spend their money at what matters most.","Teenagers who plan carefully spend their money to what matters most."],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Which paragraph of a five-paragraph argument essay contains the counterclaim and rebuttal?","answer":"paragraph 4","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الرابعة.","options":["paragraph 1","paragraph 2","paragraph 4","paragraph 5"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure"},{"q":"Which ending is a CALL TO REFLECTION?","answer":"Before you tap 'buy', ask yourself who really profits from this purchase.","explanation":"Endings.","explanation_ar":"دعوة إلى التأمل.","options":["Schools must ban phones tomorrow.","First, digital payments are fast.","Before you tap 'buy', ask yourself who really profits from this purchase.","According to SAMA, 79% of payments are electronic."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"Why should you name the source of a statistic?","answer":"so readers can judge whether the evidence is credible","explanation":"Credibility.","explanation_ar":"ليحكم القارئ على المصداقية.","options":["so readers can judge whether the evidence is credible","so the essay looks longer","so you do not need to explain it","so the counterclaim disappears"],"dok":2,"skill":"evidence"},{"q":"**SAT-style:** *Some parents prefer cash because children can see the money leave their hands. ______, prepaid cards can show the remaining balance after every purchase.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"However","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"However.","options":["However","Similarly","For example","Therefore"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"How does writing an argument essay help answer *How can smart choices about money shape our future?*","answer":"It trains us to weigh evidence and opposing views before deciding what is best.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"موازنة الأدلة والآراء.","options":["It shows that every money choice is easy.","It trains us to weigh evidence and opposing views before deciding what is best.","It teaches us to ignore other people's opinions.","It proves that only one answer is ever right."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'position': two arrows pointing in opposite directions with one highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'preview': a small window showing three upcoming icons"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'developer': a person coding a game on a computer"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reliability': a clock and a check mark beside a worker"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'canteen': students paying at a school canteen counter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'call to reflection': a person thinking with a question mark above"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'persuasive': an audience nodding during a speech"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-01","prompt":"The five money debate topics."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-02","prompt":"Learning money skills by practice."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L08-U3-LS5-03","prompt":"Students debating money issues."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V01","script":"position. Decide your position before you plan."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V02","script":"preview. The thesis gives a preview of the reasons."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V03","script":"developer. Game developers earn money from purchases."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V04","script":"reliability. A first job teaches reliability."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V05","script":"canteen. The canteen accepts digital payments."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V06","script":"call to reflection. End with a call to reflection or a call to action."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-V07","script":"persuasive. Fair counterclaims make an essay more persuasive."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-T1","script":"**Card A – Should every teenager receive a regular allowance?** · For: it gives practice in budgeting, saving and making choices; mistakes with small amounts teach lessons safely · Against: some families cannot afford it; children may waste it or expect money without effort · Fact: the OECD found that students who have worked on financial tasks at school tend to perform better in financial literacy **Card B – Should teenagers aged 15 and over be allowed to take part-time summer jobs?** · For: they earn an income, learn responsibility and build skills such as communication and time management · Against: work may tire students or take time from family, rest and summer study · Fact: many employers say that young people's first jobs teach reliability and teamwork **Card C – Should school canteens accept only digital payments?** · For: faster queues, no lost coins, and parents can track spending · Against: some students may not have a card, and digital money can feel less 'real', so students may spend more · Fact: according to the Saudi Central Bank (SAMA), electronic payments made up 79% of retail payments in Saudi Arabia in 2024, up from 70% in 2023 **Card D – Should entrepreneurship be taught in middle school?** · For: students learn to plan, take reasonable risks, solve problems and understand profit and loss · Against: the timetable is crowded; not every student wants to start a business · Fact: entrepreneurship programs often combine mathematics, writing and teamwork in real projects **Card E – Should games for children under 16 be allowed to sell in-app purchases?** · For: developers need income, and players can choose not to buy · Against: purchases can be designed to encourage impulse spending, and young players may not understand the real cost · Fact: in the OECD's PISA 2022 study, more than 85% of 15-year-olds in the participating OECD countries had bought something online in the previous year"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L08-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Allowances, summer jobs, digital canteens, young entrepreneurs, in-app purchases – pick your debate! Hook, background, claim and preview – then one reason per body paragraph, with evidence and explanation. Paragraph 4: admit, turn, rebut, link back. Restate, sum up and end with a call to action – or a call to reflection. Revise for evidence and fairness, then take the stage at the Money Matters Debate!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Rent and electricity are the family's largest monthly ___.","answer":"expenses","explanation":"Expense.","explanation_ar":"النفقات.","options":["expenses","profits","entrepreneurs","luxuries"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"A young ___ started a delivery business with two bicycles.","answer":"entrepreneur","explanation":"Entrepreneur.","explanation_ar":"رائد أعمال.","options":["consumer","debt","entrepreneur","necessity"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Saving for university became the young couple's top ___ after their son was born.","answer":"priority","explanation":"Priority.","explanation_ar":"أولوية.","options":["luxury","income","loss","priority"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The café made a ___ of 3,000 riyals in its first month, so the owners were delighted.","answer":"profit","explanation":"Profit.","explanation_ar":"ربح.","options":["loss","debt","scam","profit"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which phrase is the correct collocation?","answer":"pay off a debt","explanation":"Collocation.","explanation_ar":"pay off a debt.","options":["do a debt","pay off a debt","make off a debt","win a debt"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Although the advertisement sounded exciting, the careful shopper decided to **verify** its claims first.* *Verify* means ___.","answer":"check that something is true","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يتحقق.","options":["check that something is true","believe immediately","forget","share online"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The new rule caused a **substantial** drop in spending – almost half.* *Substantial* means ___.","answer":"large","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"كبير.","options":["tiny","large","temporary","secret"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word means *a weak, exaggerated version of an opponent's argument*?","answer":"straw man","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"رجل القش.","options":["drawback","preview","straw man","anecdote"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Many apps offer free trials. ______, many users forget to cancel before the paid subscription begins.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"Unfortunately","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"للأسف.","options":["For example","Similarly","In other words","Unfortunately"],"dok":2,"skill":"transitions","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"},{"q":"*Each of the students in the savings club ______ a notebook to record income and expenses.* Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","answer":"keeps","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement (each).","explanation_ar":"keeps.","options":["keep","keeps","are keeping","have kept"],"dok":2,"skill":"conventions","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"},{"q":"*Majed had two goals for his money ______ buying shoes and giving to charity.* Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","answer":":","explanation":"Colon before a list or explanation.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان.","options":[";",",",":","and"],"dok":3,"skill":"conventions","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"},{"q":"*Admittedly, saving takes patience ______ the rewards are worth the wait.* Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","answer":"; however,","explanation":"Punctuation with however.","explanation_ar":"; however,","options":[", however","; however,","however,","; however"],"dok":3,"skill":"conventions","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"},{"q":"*Opponents contend that teenagers are too young to work. ______, many first jobs, such as helping in a family shop, are safe, short and well supervised.* Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","answer":"Yet","explanation":"Transitions.","explanation_ar":"Yet.","options":["Therefore","For this reason","Yet","Likewise"],"dok":3,"skill":"transitions","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes: *• PISA 2022 tested 15-year-olds' financial literacy. • On average across OECD countries, 18% lacked basic financial literacy. • Saudi Arabia took part.* The student wants to emphasize that many students need help with money skills. Which choice MOST effectively uses relevant information from the notes?","answer":"In the PISA 2022 assessment, 18% of 15-year-olds in OECD countries, on average, lacked basic financial literacy.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"يبرز الحاجة.","options":["In the PISA 2022 assessment, 18% of 15-year-olds in OECD countries, on average, lacked basic financial literacy.","PISA 2022 tested 15-year-olds.","Saudi Arabia took part in PISA 2022.","PISA is an international assessment."],"dok":3,"skill":"rhetorical synthesis","_part":"Part D – SAT-style Expression of Ideas and Conventions"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a five-paragraph argument essay (250–300 words) on this claim, agreeing or disagreeing: *Children should save at least half of the money they receive as Eid gifts.* Include a hook and a claim with a preview of two reasons, two body paragraphs with evidence or examples, a fair counterclaim with a rebuttal (outweigh, refute or solve) and a conclusion with a call to action or reflection. Use at least two finance collocations.","model":"Every Eid, children's pockets fill with gifts from parents, grandparents, uncles and aunts. By the end of the week, however, most of that money has often disappeared on sweets and toys. **Children should save at least half of their Eid money, because it builds a strong saving habit and helps them reach bigger goals.** (new paragraph) First, saving half of their Eid money teaches children a habit that can last a lifetime. Eid comes every year, so the rule becomes a routine: half to spend, half to save. As the article on money choices explains, making saving automatic is one of the best ways to fight present bias – the pull of rewards today over rewards later. (new paragraph) Moreover, saved Eid money adds up to something meaningful. A child who receives 400 riyals at each Eid and saves half will put aside 400 riyals a year, because there are two Eids. Over three years, that becomes 1,200 riyals – enough for a bicycle, a tablet or a gift for the family. Small amounts, saved regularly, can make a big difference. (new paragraph) Admittedly, Eid is a celebration, and children should enjoy their gifts. Nevertheless, the benefits of saving half outweigh this concern. Children still have the other half to spend freely, and the joy of reaching a big goal later often lasts longer than the excitement of a toy that breaks after a week. (new paragraph) In conclusion, saving half of their Eid money helps children build good habits and achieve goals they could not otherwise afford. This Eid, families should make the half-and-half rule a new tradition.","marks":20}});
